const e=[{id:"exp-gokart",name:"Street Go-Karting (Mario-style)",jp:"公道カート",city:"Tokyo",category:"Entertainment",tier:2,desc:"Drive a real go-kart on Tokyo streets in costume — Shibuya Crossing, Rainbow Bridge, Akihabara. Legal, surreal, great photos.",hours:"Day & night slots, ~1–2h tours",cost:"$55–90 (¥8,800–14,400)",station:"Shibuya / Akihabara / Asakusa garages",duration:"1.5–2 hours",decNote:"HARD REQUIREMENT: a valid license + an International Driving Permit (1949 Geneva convention) — a US license alone is NOT accepted. Get the IDP from AAA BEFORE flying; you cannot get one in Japan. Night runs are coldest but the neon is the whole point — layer up."},{id:"exp-sumo-practice",name:"Sumo Morning Practice (Asageiko)",jp:"朝稽古見学",city:"Tokyo",category:"Entertainment",tier:2,desc:"Dawn stable visit: watch rikishi practice, close-range, then chanko-nabe lunch. Quiet and memorable.",hours:"Early morning (~07:00–10:00)",cost:"$55–110 (¥8,800–17,600) via tour",station:"Ryogoku",duration:"2–3 hours",decNote:"Must be booked through a tour/guide (stables don't take walk-ins, and etiquette is strict: silence, no flash, no leaving early). The half-squad that wakes for it always rates it a highlight; the rest do Ryogoku sumo town + the Edo-Tokyo museum."},{id:"exp-cooking",name:"Cooking Class — ramen, sushi or wagyu",jp:"料理教室",city:"Osaka",category:"Entertainment",tier:3,desc:"Hands-on class with an English-speaking chef: hand-pull ramen, press nigiri, or sear your own wagyu. Osaka ('Japan's kitchen') and Tokyo both have great small-group studios — you eat what you make.",hours:"Late-morning & evening slots",cost:"$55–95 (¥8,800–15,200)",station:"Namba / Dotonbori area",duration:"2–3 hours",decNote:"A great rainy-afternoon or jet-lagged-evening audible that doesn't depend on weather. Book 1–2 weeks out for a party of 8; flag allergies/vegetarians when reserving."},{id:"exp-kimono",name:"Kimono / Yukata Rental in Higashiyama",jp:"着物レンタル",city:"Kyoto",category:"Entertainment",tier:3,desc:"Rent a full kimono (with dressing + hair) and wander the preserved Higashiyama lanes — Sannenzaka, Yasaka Pagoda, Gion — in proper kit. December cold makes the haori coats and hand-warmers part of the look.",hours:"~09:00 rental, return by ~18:00",cost:"$30–60 (¥4,800–9,600)",station:"Kiyomizu-Gojo / Gion-Shijo",duration:"Half to full day",decNote:"Book morning slot. Request winter kimono + haori. Walking in geta on cobblestones is challenging."},{id:"exp-tea",name:"Tea Ceremony (Chadō)",jp:"茶道体験",city:"Kyoto",category:"Entertainment",tier:3,desc:"Guided matcha ceremony in a tatami tea room. Calm, structured experience. Some studios pair with kimono rental.",hours:"Scheduled sittings, ~45–60 min",cost:"$30–55 (¥4,800–8,800)",station:"Gion / Higashiyama",duration:"1 hour",decNote:"Small rooms — book the 8 as a private group so it's just the crew. A genuinely grounding hour mid-trip; pairs naturally with the kimono morning."},{id:"exp-helicopter",name:"Tokyo Night Helicopter Flight",jp:"ヘリ夜景フライト",city:"Tokyo",category:"Viewpoint",tier:3,desc:"A short night flight over the Tokyo sprawl — Rainbow Bridge, Tokyo Tower, the endless light grid to the horizon. December's dry, clear air is the best window of the year for it.",hours:"Evening flights",cost:"$110–200 (¥17,600–32,000) / person, shared",station:"Urayasu / Tokyo Heliport",duration:"~20 min flight (allow 2h)",decNote:"Splurge audible for the splurge-inclined; a 3-seat charter splits well. December clarity = peak visibility. Book the slot ahead and check the weather-cancel policy."},{id:"exp-sake-tasting",name:"Guided Sake Tasting Flight",jp:"日本酒テイスティング",city:"Tokyo",category:"Nightlife",tier:3,desc:"Sommelier-led tasting of 6+ sakes with food pairings. Learn junmai vs ginjo. Hot sake (atsukan) for December.",hours:"Evening sessions",cost:"$40–70 (¥6,400–11,200)",station:"Shinjuku / Shibuya",duration:"1.5 hours",decNote:"Feeds straight into the trip's sake education (Gekkeikan in Fushimi, Harushika in Nara). Book a session for the curious half of the squad; ask for hot-sake (atsukan) examples for the season."},{id:"exp-animal-cafe",name:"Animal Cafés (cat / owl / hedgehog / capybara)",jp:"動物カフェ",city:"Tokyo",category:"Entertainment",tier:3,desc:"Tokyo's themed animal cafés range from chill cat lounges to owls, hedgehogs, and even capybaras. A quick, warm, very-Tokyo curiosity stop — best treated as 30–45 minutes, not an afternoon.",hours:"~11:00–20:00, timed entry",cost:"$12–22 (¥1,920–3,520) incl. a drink",station:"Harajuku / Akihabara / Kichijoji",duration:"30–45 min",decNote:"Pick reputable, welfare-first cat cafés (clean, spacious, animals can opt out). Skip anywhere cramped or where animals look stressed — the ethics vary a lot. A fun warm-up break, not a headline."},{id:"exp-samurai-ninja",name:"Samurai & Ninja Experience",jp:"侍・忍者体験",city:"Kyoto",category:"Entertainment",tier:3,desc:"Live sword demos, hands-on katana, ninja star throwing, museum. Fun group activity between Nishiki and Gion.",hours:"~10:00–19:00, timed sessions",cost:"$22–40 (¥3,520–6,400)",station:"Sanjo / near Nishiki",duration:"1–1.5 hours",decNote:"Book a session slot for the 8; the star-throwing leaderboard inevitably becomes its own faction war. Indoor — a solid rain-plan audible for a Kyoto afternoon."},{id:"exp-day-onsen",name:"Day Onsen / Super-Sento Spa",jp:"日帰り温泉",city:"Tokyo",category:"Entertainment",tier:2,desc:"A half-day at a big bathhouse complex — indoor/outdoor baths, saunas, cold plunge, relaxation lounges. The cold-weather recovery move after a 25,000-step day; some run 24h as a last-train bivouac.",hours:"Many open ~11:00 to late / 24h",cost:"$8–28 (¥1,280–4,480)",station:"Odaiba / Shinjuku / various",duration:"2–4 hours",decNote:"Outdoor bath in winter cold is intense. Confirm tattoo policy first or book private kashikiri; Thermae-Yu Shinjuku is tattoo-friendly."},{id:"sensoji",name:"Senso-ji Temple",jp:"浅草寺",city:"Tokyo",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:1,desc:"Tokyo's oldest temple (645 AD) behind the giant red Kaminarimon lantern. The approach through Nakamise's snack stalls is half the experience. Draw an omikuji fortune — bad ones get tied to the rack and left behind.",hours:"Grounds 24h; main hall 6:00–17:00",cost:"Free",station:"Asakusa (Ginza/Asakusa lines)",duration:"1.5–2h",decNote:"Gorgeous at dawn or after dark when lit up and empty — stalls close ~18:00. Late Dec the Hagoita-ichi battledore market fills the grounds."},{id:"meiji",name:"Meiji Jingu Shrine",jp:"明治神宮",city:"Tokyo",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:1,desc:"A 100,000-tree forest hiding Tokyo's grandest Shinto shrine, steps from Harajuku station. Giant torii gates, sake barrel walls, and frequent traditional wedding processions on weekend mornings.",hours:"Sunrise–sunset (~6:40–16:20 in Dec)",cost:"Free",station:"Harajuku (JR Yamanote)",duration:"1–1.5h",decNote:"Late December = New Year prep; the shrine gears up for ~3 million hatsumode visitors. Closes at dusk — go before the ~16:30 sunset."},{id:"shibuya-crossing",name:"Shibuya Scramble Crossing",jp:"渋谷スクランブル交差点",city:"Tokyo",category:"Landmark",tier:1,desc:"Up to 3,000 people cross at once at the world's busiest intersection. Cross it, then watch from above — Mag's Park rooftop (cheap) or the Starbucks in Tsutaya (free with coffee).",hours:"24h — best after dark",cost:"Free",station:"Shibuya (Hachiko exit)",duration:"30 min",decNote:"December nights add Christmas ads to the giant screens, and the Aoyama-dori illumination glows two blocks over. Pure Blade Runner."},{id:"shibuya-sky",name:"Shibuya Sky",jp:"渋谷スカイ",city:"Tokyo",category:"Viewpoint",tier:1,desc:"Open-air rooftop 229m up with Tokyo's best 360° view — glass corners, net hammocks, and the crossing directly below. Beats Skytree on vibes despite being half the height.",hours:"10:00–22:30 (last entry 21:20)",cost:"$17 (¥2,700) day / $21 (¥3,400) after 15:00",station:"Shibuya (direct access)",duration:"1.5h",decNote:"Book the 15:30–16:00 slot: with the ~16:30 December sunset you get daylight, golden hour AND night skyline with a clear Fuji silhouette. Sells out 2 weeks ahead."},{id:"teamlab",name:"teamLab Planets",jp:"チームラボプラネッツ",city:"Tokyo",category:"Museum & Art",tier:1,desc:"Walk barefoot through knee-deep water as koi made of light swim around your legs. Infinity mirror rooms, a floating flower garden, and the most Instagrammed museum on Earth — deservedly. Confirmed open through 2027.",hours:"9:00–22:00 (varies)",cost:"$24 (¥3,800) weekday / $26 (¥4,200) weekend",station:"Shin-Toyosu (Yurikamome)",duration:"2–2.5h",decNote:"Book on the official DMM store 2–3 weeks out; morning slots vanish first. Wear pants you can roll up — the water is warmed, the December walk to the door is not."},{id:"teamlab-borderless",name:"teamLab Borderless (Azabudai Hills)",jp:"チームラボボーダレス",city:"Tokyo",category:"Museum & Art",tier:1,desc:"The 'borderless' sibling reborn at Azabudai Hills: artworks escape their rooms and roam the halls, the lamp room does its infinite-mirror thing, and there's no map on purpose. A different beast from Planets — no water, more wandering, equally unreal.",hours:"~9:00–21:00 (varies)",cost:"$24–30 (¥3,800–4,800), date-priced",station:"Kamiyacho (Hibiya line)",duration:"2.5–3h",decNote:"Book 2–4 weeks out. Tokyo Tower looms next door lit up for winter — stack them into one evening."},{id:"skytree",name:"Tokyo Skytree",jp:"東京スカイツリー",city:"Tokyo",category:"Viewpoint",tier:2,desc:"634m — the tallest tower in the world. Two observation decks; the lower Tembo Deck honestly has 90% of the view. Solamachi mall below is a solid souvenir + dinner stop.",hours:"10:00–22:00",cost:"$13–21 (¥2,100–3,400)",station:"Tokyo Skytree (Tobu)/Oshiage",duration:"1.5–2h",decNote:"December air is the year's clearest — real Fuji chances at the ~16:30 sunset. Tower runs a winter illumination color scheme all month."},{id:"tokyo-tower",name:"Tokyo Tower",jp:"東京タワー",city:"Tokyo",category:"Landmark",tier:2,desc:"The 1958 orange-and-white icon. Arguably better from the outside than the inside — shoot it from Shiba Park or the Zojo-ji temple grounds where it looms over the temple roof.",hours:"9:00–23:00",cost:"$9 (¥1,500) main deck (free to admire)",station:"Akabanebashi (Oedo line)",duration:"1h",decNote:"Special winter light-up; the Roppongi Hills Keyakizaka view of it framed by Christmas lights is the classic December shot."},{id:"tsukiji",name:"Tsukiji Outer Market",jp:"築地場外市場",city:"Tokyo",category:"Market & Shopping",tier:1,desc:"The inner wholesale market moved to Toyosu, but the outer market remains Tokyo's best breakfast: tamagoyaki on sticks, scallop skewers, uni bowls, knife shops, and 400 stalls of organized chaos.",hours:"5:00–14:00 (go before 10)",cost:"Free entry; $13–25 (¥2,000–4,000) to eat well",station:"Tsukiji (Hibiya line)",duration:"1.5–2h",decNote:"December = peak crab and oyster season — grilled scallops and fresh oysters everywhere. Some stalls close Sundays; late Dec it's mobbed with New Year shoppers."},{id:"toyosu",name:"Toyosu Market & Senkyaku Banrai",jp:"豊洲市場",city:"Tokyo",category:"Market & Shopping",tier:2,desc:"Where the famous tuna auction actually moved. Watch the bidding from the observation deck (free), then eat sushi-breakfast at one of the wholesalers' counters. The adjoining Senkyaku Banrai complex adds an Edo-style food street and a rooftop hot-spring foot bath over the bay.",hours:"Market ~5:00–15:00, closed Sun & some Weds",cost:"Free entry; sushi sets $19–44 (¥3,000–7,000)",station:"Shijo-mae (Yurikamome)",duration:"2–3h",decNote:"Tuna auction viewing is the most reliable in the slow late-December stretch. Pair the rooftop onsen foot bath with a near-freezing dawn — perfect logic."},{id:"akihabara",name:"Akihabara Electric Town",jp:"秋葉原",city:"Tokyo",category:"Entertainment",tier:1,desc:"Anime, retro games, electronics, and arcades stacked eight floors high. Super Potato for retro game treasure, GiGO and Taito for crane games, Yodobashi for everything with a plug. Mandarake's complex is eight floors of secondhand otaku archaeology.",hours:"Shops ~10:00–20:00, arcades till 23:00+",cost:"Free to browse; your wallet decides",station:"Akihabara (JR Yamanote)",duration:"2–4h",decNote:"Year-end sales kick off late December — electronics and figures get genuinely cheaper. Buy big-ticket plugs here for the tax-free counter."},{id:"ueno",name:"Ueno Park & Museums",jp:"上野公園",city:"Tokyo",category:"Park & Nature",tier:2,desc:"Tokyo's museum quarter: the Tokyo National Museum (samurai armor, swords, national treasures), science museum, zoo with pandas, and Ameyoko market street running south to Okachimachi.",hours:"Park 24h; museums ~9:30–17:00, most closed Mon",cost:"TNM $6 (¥1,000); park free",station:"Ueno (JR Yamanote)",duration:"2–4h",decNote:"Ameyoko market goes feral in late December as locals shop for New Year feasts — crab legs and bargain shouting. Bare ginkgo trees, freezing benches."},{id:"harajuku",name:"Takeshita Street, Harajuku",jp:"竹下通り",city:"Tokyo",category:"Market & Shopping",tier:2,desc:"400m of maximalist teen fashion, rainbow crepes, purikura photo booths, and sensory overload. Walk it once for the spectacle, then escape to Cat Street's vintage shops toward Shibuya.",hours:"Shops ~10:00–20:00",cost:"Free; crepes ~$4 (¥600)",station:"Harajuku (JR Yamanote)",duration:"1–2h",decNote:"Brutal crowds on weekend afternoons — go weekday morning. Omotesando's champagne-gold illumination runs two minutes away."},{id:"omotesando",name:"Omotesando & Cat Street",jp:"表参道",city:"Tokyo",category:"Market & Shopping",tier:2,desc:"Tokyo's Champs-Élysées: zelkova-lined avenue of flagship architecture (Prada's glass bubble building, Tod's, Dior). Cat Street behind it has the vintage and streetwear gems.",hours:"Shops 11:00–20:00",cost:"Free to gawk",station:"Omotesando (Ginza line)",duration:"1–2h",decNote:"The champagne-gold illumination along the whole avenue is a December highlight — best after the ~16:30 sunset."},{id:"shinjuku-gyoen",name:"Shinjuku Gyoen Garden",jp:"新宿御苑",city:"Tokyo",category:"Park & Nature",tier:2,desc:"58 hectares of Japanese, French, and English gardens five minutes from the world's busiest train station. The greenhouse is a free tropical escape when December bites.",hours:"9:00–16:30, closed Mon",cost:"$3 (¥500)",station:"Shinjuku-gyoemmae (Marunouchi)",duration:"1.5h",decNote:"Some maple color lingers into early December; quiet and frosty otherwise. The heated greenhouse is the move on a near-freezing dawn."},{id:"golden-gai",name:"Golden Gai",jp:"ゴールデン街",city:"Tokyo",category:"Nightlife",tier:1,desc:"Six alleys, 200+ bars, most seating 5–8 people. Each bar is its own tiny universe — punk vinyl, horror movies, jazz. Look for 'no cover' or English signs if shy; cover charges buy you a seat in someone's world.",hours:"~20:00–late",cost:"Cover $0–9 (¥0–1,500) + drinks from $4 (¥700)",station:"Shinjuku East exit",duration:"2–4h",decNote:"Cold December nights make these tiny warm rooms even better. Split into 2s/3s — eight bodies won't fit in one bar."},{id:"omoide",name:"Omoide Yokocho",jp:"思い出横丁",city:"Tokyo",category:"Nightlife",tier:2,desc:"'Memory Lane' (politely) — a smoky alley of postage-stamp yakitori joints under the train tracks. Sit elbow-to-elbow with salarymen, point at skewers, repeat. Cash only at most counters.",hours:"~17:00–24:00",cost:"$13–19 (¥2,000–3,000) per person",station:"Shinjuku West exit",duration:"1–2h",decNote:"Steam, smoke, and grill-glow in winter air = the most cinematic dinner in Tokyo. Order the motsuni (offal stew) — it exists for December."},{id:"kabukicho",name:"Kabukicho & Godzilla Head",jp:"歌舞伎町",city:"Tokyo",category:"Nightlife",tier:3,desc:"Neon-soaked entertainment district. See the life-size Godzilla looming over the Toho cinema, hit a mega arcade or the Kabukicho Tower's basement food hall. Ignore street touts — never follow one into a bar.",hours:"Always on",cost:"Free to wander",station:"Seibu-Shinjuku / Shinjuku East",duration:"1h",decNote:"Coldest hours thin the touts. The Tokyu Kabukicho Tower's Shinjuku Kabuki Hall food court is a warm, sane place to regroup."},{id:"ghibli",name:"Ghibli Museum",jp:"三鷹の森ジブリ美術館",city:"Tokyo",category:"Museum & Art",tier:1,desc:"Miyazaki's hand-built wonderland in Mitaka: original animation cells, a giant Catbus room, the rooftop Laputa robot, and an exclusive short film you can't see anywhere else. No photos inside — by design, you just experience it.",hours:"10:00–18:00, closed Tue",cost:"$6 (¥1,000) adults",station:"Mitaka (JR Chuo) + bus/walk",duration:"2–3h",decNote:"December tickets sell out in minutes when they drop on the 10th of the prior month, 10:00 JST, via Lawson. All 8 of you should try at once."},{id:"disneysea",name:"Tokyo DisneySea",jp:"東京ディズニーシー",city:"Tokyo",category:"Entertainment",tier:2,desc:"The only DisneySea on Earth and widely called the best Disney park, period. Nautical steampunk theming, Fantasy Springs (Frozen/Tangled/Peter Pan), and alcohol — yes, it serves beer.",hours:"9:00–21:00",cost:"$49–68 (¥7,900–10,900), dated ticket",station:"Maihama (JR Keiyo) + monorail",duration:"Full day",decNote:"Christmas event with themed parades runs through Dec 25, then pivots to New Year decor. Weekday > weekend, always."},{id:"pokemon-center",name:"Pokémon Center & Nintendo Tokyo",jp:"ポケモンセンター",city:"Tokyo",category:"Market & Shopping",tier:2,desc:"Shibuya Parco's 6th floor is nerd heaven: Pokémon Center with a life-size Mewtwo in a tank, Nintendo Tokyo, and a Capcom store side by side. Exclusive merch you literally cannot get elsewhere.",hours:"10:00–21:00",cost:"Free entry",station:"Shibuya (8 min walk)",duration:"1–1.5h",decNote:"Holiday-exclusive plush drops in December. Tax-free counter upstairs — bring your passport."},{id:"imperial-palace",name:"Imperial Palace East Gardens",jp:"皇居東御苑",city:"Tokyo",category:"Park & Nature",tier:3,desc:"The shogun's old castle grounds — massive stone walls, the foundation of Edo Castle's keep, and manicured gardens. A calm hour between Tokyo Station and Marunouchi's lights.",hours:"9:00–16:00, closed Mon/Fri",cost:"Free",station:"Otemachi / Tokyo Stn",duration:"1–1.5h",decNote:"Pair with the Marunouchi illumination after — exit toward Tokyo Station as the ~16:30 sun drops."},{id:"yanaka",name:"Yanaka Old Town",jp:"谷中",city:"Tokyo",category:"Landmark",tier:3,desc:"The Tokyo that survived the war: wooden houses, tiny temples, cats everywhere, and Yanaka Ginza's old-school shopping street. The antidote to Shibuya. Best on a slow morning.",hours:"Shops ~10:00–18:00",cost:"Free",station:"Nippori (JR Yamanote)",duration:"2h",decNote:"Yanaka Ginza's 'sunset stairs' face west — time the ~16:30 winter sunset and grab a menchi-katsu for warmth."},{id:"nezu-shrine",name:"Nezu Shrine",jp:"根津神社",city:"Tokyo",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:3,desc:"One of Tokyo's oldest shrines — the 1705 buildings are all original, having dodged both the 1923 quake and the war. Vermillion halls, koi ponds, and a hillside tunnel of mini torii that's Fushimi Inari in miniature, with roughly 0.1% of the crowd.",hours:"~6:00–17:00 (Dec)",cost:"Free",station:"Nezu (Chiyoda line)",duration:"45 min",decNote:"Ten minutes' walk from Yanaka — pair them into one slow, old-Tokyo morning. Closes at the ~16:30 dusk."},{id:"shimokita",name:"Shimokitazawa",jp:"下北沢",city:"Tokyo",category:"Market & Shopping",tier:3,desc:"Tokyo's Brooklyn: vintage clothing dens, record stores, curry restaurants (a local obsession), and small live houses. Where Tokyo twentysomethings actually hang out.",hours:"Shops 12:00–20:00",cost:"Free to browse",station:"Shimokitazawa (Odakyu/Keio)",duration:"2–3h",decNote:"8 min from Shibuya. The Reload and Mikan complexes string up holiday lights; warm curry counters are the winter draw."},{id:"roppongi",name:"Roppongi Hills & Mori Art Museum",jp:"六本木ヒルズ",city:"Tokyo",category:"Museum & Art",tier:2,desc:"Contemporary art museum on the 53rd floor with a city view included — art and skyline in one ticket. The Keyakizaka illumination below frames Tokyo Tower perfectly.",hours:"10:00–22:00 (museum)",cost:"$13 (¥2,000), incl. observation",station:"Roppongi (Hibiya line)",duration:"2–3h",decNote:"Keyakizaka's blue-white LED tunnel with Tokyo Tower behind it is THE December photo — lights on at dusk through Christmas."},{id:"tokyo-station",name:"Tokyo Station & Marunouchi Illumination",jp:"東京駅・丸の内",city:"Tokyo",category:"Landmark",tier:3,desc:"The 1914 red-brick station restored to full glory, the free KITTE rooftop view over the shinkansen throat, and Naka-dori avenue glowing champagne-gold for the holidays. Tokyo's classiest 60 minutes, and it costs nothing.",hours:"Illumination ~15:00–23:00; KITTE roof 11:00–22:00",cost:"Free",station:"Tokyo Stn (Marunouchi side)",duration:"1h",decNote:"December IS the point — one of Tokyo's best illuminations, zero queue, zero ticket. Where you'll catch the SmartEX shinkansen south anyway."},{id:"kappabashi",name:"Kappabashi Kitchen Town",jp:"かっぱ橋道具街",city:"Tokyo",category:"Market & Shopping",tier:3,desc:"800 meters of restaurant-supply street between Ueno and Asakusa: hand-forged knife shops that engrave your blade while you wait, and fake-food showrooms selling wax sushi magnets and a plastic ramen that defies physics.",hours:"~9:00–17:00 (many shops close Sun)",cost:"Gawking free; good knives from $50 (¥8,000)",station:"Tawaramachi (Ginza line)",duration:"1–1.5h",decNote:"A Japanese kitchen knife beats any airport souvenir. Checked bag YES, carry-on NO."},{id:"inokashira",name:"Inokashira Park & Kichijoji",jp:"井の頭公園・吉祥寺",city:"Tokyo",category:"Park & Nature",tier:3,desc:"The park that hides the Ghibli Museum: swan pedal-boats on the pond (legend says couples who ride together break up — eight dudes are immune), winter birds, and Kichijoji's Harmonica Yokocho bar maze at the north exit.",hours:"Park 24h",cost:"Free; swan boats ~$5 (¥800)/30 min",station:"Kichijoji (JR Chuo)",duration:"1.5–2h",decNote:"The mandatory pairing if the Ghibli ticket gods smile; Harmonica Yokocho's micro-bars light up and warm up at the ~16:30 dusk."},{id:"sengakuji",name:"Sengaku-ji (47 Ronin)",jp:"泉岳寺",city:"Tokyo",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:3,desc:"The graves of the 47 Ronin, who avenged their lord in 1702 knowing it meant their own deaths — one of history's greatest loyalty stories, at its source. Small museum, incense-wreathed grave rows, heavy quiet.",hours:"7:00–17:00; museum 9:00–16:00",cost:"Grounds free; museum $3 (¥500)",station:"Sengakuji (Asakusa line)",duration:"45 min–1h",decNote:"The Gishisai memorial festival falls on Dec 14 — the day we land. The incense smoke lingers all month."},{id:"odaiba",name:"Odaiba & teamLab Bayside",jp:"お台場",city:"Tokyo",category:"Entertainment",tier:3,desc:"Reclaimed bay island with a 1:1 scale moving Unicorn Gundam outside DiverCity, a replica Statue of Liberty, big malls, and Rainbow Bridge views back at the skyline. A lazy half-day when the group needs malls and arcades indoors.",hours:"Malls 11:00–21:00; Gundam outdoor 24h",cost:"Free to wander",station:"Odaiba-Kaihinkoen (Yurikamome)",duration:"Half day",decNote:"Rainbow Bridge gets a special winter light-up and the bayfront is wide open for clear Fuji-at-sunset views across the water — bring the windproof layer, it's brutal out there."},{id:"fushimi",name:"Fushimi Inari Taisha",jp:"伏見稲荷大社",city:"Kyoto",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:1,desc:"10,000 vermillion torii gates snaking up a sacred mountain. The full loop takes 2–3 hours; most people stop at the Yotsutsuji viewpoint (~45 min up) where the gates keep going and the crowds don't.",hours:"24h — yes, you can go at dawn or midnight",cost:"Free",station:"Inari (JR Nara line, 2 stops)",duration:"1.5–3h",decNote:"Sunrise in December (~6:50) means you can beat the tour buses without waking at 5. Bring gloves — the upper trail is near-freezing at dawn."},{id:"kinkakuji",name:"Kinkaku-ji (Golden Pavilion)",jp:"金閣寺",city:"Kyoto",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:1,desc:"A Zen pavilion wrapped in actual gold leaf floating over a mirror pond. You've seen the photo; it's better in person, especially in low winter sun. The viewing path takes 30 minutes — go for the moment, not the duration.",hours:"9:00–17:00",cost:"$3 (¥500)",station:"Bus 101/205 from Kyoto Stn",duration:"45 min–1h",decNote:"If it snows even once while we're there, DROP EVERYTHING and come here — golden pavilion under snow is the single best winter sight in Japan."},{id:"kiyomizu",name:"Kiyomizu-dera",jp:"清水寺",city:"Kyoto",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:1,desc:"A massive wooden stage cantilevered off a hillside, built without a single nail. Drink from the Otowa waterfall's three streams (love, school, longevity — pick ONE, greedy gets nothing) and descend through Higashiyama's lantern-lit lanes.",hours:"6:00–18:00",cost:"$3 (¥500)",station:"Bus to Kiyomizu-michi + walk",duration:"1.5–2h",decNote:"December = no crowds by Kyoto standards. The view over the city is winter-crisp. Check for a special evening illumination — some Dec dates open the gates after dark."},{id:"arashiyama",name:"Arashiyama Bamboo Grove",jp:"嵐山竹林",city:"Kyoto",category:"Park & Nature",tier:1,desc:"The famous green corridor of towering bamboo. It's short — 500m — so the magic is entirely crowd-dependent: at 7:30am it's a cathedral, at noon it's a queue. Pair with Tenryu-ji and the river.",hours:"24h",cost:"Free",station:"Saga-Arashiyama (JR) / Randen tram",duration:"30 min (grove) / half day (area)",decNote:"Winter morning light through bamboo + nobody around = the photo of the trip. Mid-Dec the Arashiyama Hanatouro lantern event sometimes lights the grove at night."},{id:"tenryuji",name:"Tenryu-ji Temple",jp:"天龍寺",city:"Kyoto",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:2,desc:"UNESCO Zen temple whose 700-year-old garden borrows Arashiyama's mountains as scenery. The garden survives from 1339 — the buildings burned eight times, the landscaping never did.",hours:"8:30–17:00",cost:"$3 (¥500) garden / +$2 (¥300) buildings",station:"Arashiyama (Randen)",duration:"1h",decNote:"The back gate empties straight into the bamboo grove — enter Tenryu-ji early, exit into an empty grove."},{id:"monkey-park",name:"Iwatayama Monkey Park",jp:"嵐山モンキーパーク",city:"Kyoto",category:"Park & Nature",tier:2,desc:"120 wild Japanese macaques at the top of a 20-minute hill hike, with all of Kyoto spread below. Inside the hut, humans go in the cage and feed monkeys through the wire — a perfect inversion.",hours:"9:00–16:00 (winter)",cost:"$4 (¥600)",station:"Arashiyama, south side of bridge",duration:"1.5h",decNote:"December monkeys are extra fluffy. This is scientific fact. The climb warms you up on a cold morning."},{id:"gion",name:"Gion & Hanamikoji Street",jp:"祇園",city:"Kyoto",category:"Landmark",tier:1,desc:"Kyoto's geisha district of wooden machiya teahouses. Real geiko and maiko hurry to appointments around dusk — admire from distance, never block or chase (fines are real now). Shirakawa canal lane is the prettiest 200m in the city.",hours:"Best 16:30–19:00",cost:"Free to wander",station:"Gion-Shijo (Keihan)",duration:"1–2h",decNote:"Early-dusk lantern light (the ~16:30 sunset helps) + bare willows over the canal = peak atmosphere."},{id:"yasaka-shrine",name:"Yasaka Shrine",jp:"八坂神社",city:"Kyoto",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:2,desc:"Gion's guardian shrine at the top of Shijo-dori, open all night: the central stage drips with white lanterns that glow after dark, and the grounds spill straight into Maruyama Park. The natural finale of a Gion evening walk.",hours:"24h",cost:"Free",station:"Gion-Shijo (Keihan)",duration:"30–45 min",decNote:"Late December = crews staging the okera-mairi New Year fire rite. Lanterns + cold air = the night photo of Kyoto."},{id:"nishiki",name:"Nishiki Market",jp:"錦市場",city:"Kyoto",category:"Market & Shopping",tier:1,desc:"'Kyoto's Kitchen' — 400 years old, 130 stalls, five blocks: tako tamago (candied octopus with a quail egg head), yuba, pickles, knives, matcha everything. Eat standing at each stall; walking-while-eating is the one rule.",hours:"~10:00–17:00 (stalls vary)",cost:"$13–19 (¥2,000–3,000) grazing",station:"Shijo (Karasuma) / Kawaramachi",duration:"1–1.5h",decNote:"Late December: locals shopping for osechi (New Year food) make it wonderfully chaotic. Covered arcade = warm shelter from the cold."},{id:"ginkakuji",name:"Ginkaku-ji (Silver Pavilion)",jp:"銀閣寺",city:"Kyoto",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:2,desc:"Never actually silvered — and better for it. Wabi-sabi perfection: a raked sand garden with a Fuji-shaped cone, moss hillsides, and restraint that makes Kinkaku-ji look like it's showing off.",hours:"9:00–16:30 (winter)",cost:"$3 (¥500)",station:"Bus 5/17 to Ginkakuji-michi",duration:"1h",decNote:"Frost on the moss garden in the early hours is a quiet, underrated December scene."},{id:"philosophers",name:"Philosopher's Path",jp:"哲学の道",city:"Kyoto",category:"Park & Nature",tier:3,desc:"2km canal-side walk from Ginkaku-ji toward Nanzen-ji, named for a Kyoto University philosopher's daily stroll. Cherry trees are bare in December but the stone path, cats, and small temples keep it worthwhile.",hours:"24h",cost:"Free",station:"Start at Ginkaku-ji",duration:"45 min walk",decNote:"Bare and silent in winter — the calm connector between Ginkaku-ji and Nanzen-ji, not a destination itself."},{id:"nanzenji",name:"Nanzen-ji & Aqueduct",jp:"南禅寺",city:"Kyoto",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:2,desc:"Hulking Sanmon gate you can climb, Zen rock gardens, and — unexpectedly — a handsome Meiji-era brick aqueduct straight out of Rome running through the temple grounds.",hours:"8:40–16:30",cost:"Grounds free; gate $4 (¥600)",station:"Keage (Tozai line)",duration:"1h",decNote:"Subtemple Tenju-an opens for a special winter view some years — check the gate for the schedule."},{id:"nijo",name:"Nijo Castle",jp:"二条城",city:"Kyoto",category:"Landmark",tier:2,desc:"The shogun's Kyoto residence with 'nightingale floors' that chirp underfoot — a 17th-century intruder alarm. Lavish painted screens inside; massive moats and gardens outside.",hours:"8:45–16:00 (in by), closed some Tue",cost:"$8 (¥1,300) castle+palace",station:"Nijojo-mae (Tozai)",duration:"1.5–2h",decNote:"Some Decembers run a projection-mapping night event on the walls and gardens — a rare way to see the castle lit."},{id:"sanjusangendo",name:"Sanjusangen-do",jp:"三十三間堂",city:"Kyoto",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:2,desc:"Japan's longest wooden building containing 1,001 life-size golden Kannon statues in ranked rows, guarded by 28 thunder-faced deities. No photos — which is why nobody talks about it and everybody who goes is stunned.",hours:"9:00–16:00 (winter)",cost:"$4 (¥600)",station:"Shichijo (Keihan)",duration:"45 min",decNote:"The unsung hero of Kyoto. Quiet, warm-ish indoors, and jaw-dropping when December keeps the crowds away."},{id:"tofukuji",name:"Tofuku-ji Temple",jp:"東福寺",city:"Kyoto",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:3,desc:"Famous for autumn maples, but its checkerboard moss garden (Hojo Garden) is a modernist masterpiece year-round. One stop from Fushimi Inari — easy pairing.",hours:"9:00–16:00 (winter)",cost:"$3 (¥500) garden",station:"Tofukuji (JR/Keihan)",duration:"1h",decNote:"Mid-December the last maples cling on along Tsutenkyo bridge, with a fraction of the November mob."},{id:"kyoto-gosho",name:"Kyoto Imperial Palace",jp:"京都御所",city:"Kyoto",category:"Landmark",tier:3,desc:"Home of the emperors for 500 years until Tokyo poached the job in 1869. Free entry, no booking anymore: ceremonial halls, vast white-gravel courtyards, and the surrounding Gyoen park's ancient pines.",hours:"9:00–16:00 (last entry ~15:20), closed Mon",cost:"Free",station:"Imadegawa (Karasuma line)",duration:"1–1.5h",decNote:"Frosty December mornings here are dead quiet — the crowd-free hour Kyoto rarely gives you."},{id:"nishi-honganji",name:"Nishi Hongan-ji",jp:"西本願寺",city:"Kyoto",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:3,desc:"UNESCO headquarters of Shin Buddhism, ten minutes' walk from Kyoto Station: two colossal halls with acres of tatami you cross in socks, and Momoyama-era gates dripping with carving. Free, vast, and somehow on nobody's itinerary.",hours:"5:30–17:00",cost:"Free",station:"Kyoto Stn (10 min walk)",duration:"45 min–1h",decNote:"Morning services with full chanting are open to anyone — a free, warm, goosebump start before the day's first train."},{id:"uji",name:"Uji — Matcha Town & Byodo-in",jp:"宇治",city:"Kyoto",category:"Landmark",tier:2,desc:"Japan's matcha capital, 20 min south of Kyoto. Byodo-in temple is on the ¥10 coin; the tea shops grind their own stone-milled matcha. Conveniently next door to the Nintendo Museum.",hours:"Byodo-in 8:30–17:30",cost:"$4 (¥700) Byodo-in",station:"Uji (JR Nara line)",duration:"2–3h",decNote:"Pair with the Nintendo Museum (book ahead) — hot matcha and a warm parfait between the two when it's near-freezing out."},{id:"nintendo-museum",name:"Nintendo Museum",jp:"ニンテンドーミュージアム",city:"Kyoto",category:"Museum & Art",tier:1,desc:"Nintendo's own museum in Uji, opened late 2024 on the site of an old factory: the entire history from hanafuda cards to Switch, giant playable controllers, a build-your-own-Hanafuda workshop, and a Mario-burger café. Strictly lottery ticketing — reserve months out.",hours:"10:00–18:00, closed Tue",cost:"$22 (¥3,500) adults",station:"Ogura (Kintetsu) / Uji area",duration:"2.5–3h",decNote:"Tickets are a monthly lottery on the official site — every one of the 8 of you should enter for the same Kyoto-leg date. Stack it with Uji matcha for a full day south."},{id:"dotonbori",name:"Dotonbori",jp:"道頓堀",city:"Osaka",category:"Landmark",tier:1,desc:"Osaka's neon riverfront: the Glico running man, a six-meter mechanical crab, octopus lanterns, and food stalls in every direction. Loud, greasy, glorious — the anti-Kyoto, and proud of it.",hours:"Always on; best 18:00–23:00",cost:"Free (until you smell takoyaki)",station:"Namba (multiple lines)",duration:"2–3h",decNote:"Cold air + neon reflections on the canal = December Dotonbori hits harder. Crab restaurants (Kani Doraku) are in full season."},{id:"usj",name:"Universal Studios Japan",jp:"ユニバーサル・スタジオ・ジャパン",city:"Osaka",category:"Entertainment",tier:1,desc:"Super Nintendo World (Mario Kart AR ride, Power-Up Bands, Donkey Kong Country) plus the Wizarding World's snow-capped Hogsmeade. The Christmas season show is one of Japan's best theme park productions.",hours:"~8:30–21:00 (varies daily)",cost:"From $56 (¥8,900) dated ticket; Express Pass extra",station:"Universal City (JR Yumesaki)",duration:"Full day",decNote:"The Universal Christmas tree show runs through Dec 25. Holiday week is packed — Express Pass is not optional, it's survival. Book by October."},{id:"osaka-castle",name:"Osaka Castle",jp:"大阪城",city:"Osaka",category:"Landmark",tier:2,desc:"Hideyoshi's mighty fortress rebuilt in ferroconcrete — historians sniff, but the golden tigers, massive stone walls, and moat views deliver. The museum inside covers the siege that ended the samurai wars.",hours:"9:00–17:00",cost:"$4 (¥600) castle keep",station:"Osakajokoen (JR Loop)",duration:"2h",decNote:"The Osaka Castle Illuminage light festival in the park runs all December — castle + lights in one stop after the ~16:50 sunset."},{id:"umeda-sky",name:"Umeda Sky Building",jp:"梅田スカイビル",city:"Osaka",category:"Viewpoint",tier:2,desc:"Twin towers joined by a 'Floating Garden' rooftop ring 173m up, reached by a glass escalator across the void. The open-air deck at sunset over Osaka's sprawl is the best $13 in Kansai.",hours:"9:30–22:30",cost:"$13 (¥2,000)",station:"Osaka/Umeda (10 min walk)",duration:"1–1.5h",decNote:"December sunset ~16:50 — time it, then descend to the basement 'Takimi Koji' retro food alley and the winter market at the base."},{id:"shinsekai",name:"Shinsekai & Tsutenkaku",jp:"新世界",city:"Osaka",category:"Landmark",tier:2,desc:"Retro 1912 entertainment district under the Tsutenkaku tower — kushikatsu joints, pachinko clatter, and Billiken the god of luck (rub his feet). Gritty in the best way. New tower slide (Tower Slider) spirals you down from the mid-deck.",hours:"Always on; kushikatsu till late",cost:"Free; tower $6 (¥1,000), slide extra",station:"Ebisucho / Dobutsuen-mae",duration:"1.5–2h",decNote:"Steaming kushikatsu oil and neon on a cold night is peak Shinsekai. Remember the one rule: no double-dipping the sauce."},{id:"kuromon",name:"Kuromon Ichiba Market",jp:"黒門市場",city:"Osaka",category:"Market & Shopping",tier:1,desc:"'Osaka's Kitchen' — 150 stalls of grilled scallops, wagyu skewers, fugu, uni, and snow crab you point at and they grill. Pricier than it was, still worth every yen at 10am with an empty stomach.",hours:"~9:00–17:00",cost:"$19–31 (¥3,000–5,000) to feast",station:"Nippombashi (5 min)",duration:"1–1.5h",decNote:"December crab season peaks here. Split a grilled king crab leg 8 ways — or don't share, that's between you and your conscience."},{id:"kaiyukan",name:"Osaka Aquarium Kaiyukan",jp:"海遊館",city:"Osaka",category:"Entertainment",tier:2,desc:"One of the world's great aquariums, built around a colossal central tank with whale sharks. You spiral down eight floors alongside it. The neighboring Tempozan Ferris wheel adds a harbor view.",hours:"10:00–20:00",cost:"$17 (¥2,700)",station:"Osakako (Chuo line)",duration:"2–3h",decNote:"Great near-freezing-day card. Winter illumination show outside the building and on the Ferris wheel after dark."},{id:"abeno-harukas",name:"Abeno Harukas 300",jp:"あべのハルカス",city:"Osaka",category:"Viewpoint",tier:2,desc:"Kansai's tallest skyscraper (300m) with a three-floor observatory: glass from carpet to ceiling, an open-air inner court, and the full sweep from Osaka Castle to the Seto Inland Sea. The southside answer to Umeda Sky.",hours:"9:00–22:00",cost:"$13 (¥2,000)",station:"Tennoji (direct access)",duration:"1–1.5h",decNote:"Five minutes from Shinsekai — stack it at the ~16:50 sunset, then drop down to Tsutenkaku and kushikatsu."},{id:"amerikamura",name:"Amerikamura",jp:"アメリカ村",city:"Osaka",category:"Market & Shopping",tier:3,desc:"Osaka's youth-culture block — streetwear, vintage, record shops, and a tiny Statue of Liberty on a roof. The Kansai answer to Harajuku, with more attitude and cheaper racks.",hours:"Shops 11:00–20:00",cost:"Free to browse",station:"Shinsaibashi (5 min)",duration:"1–2h",decNote:"Triangle Park people-watching even in the cold; 10 minutes' walk to Den Den Town for the nerd contingent."},{id:"denden",name:"Den Den Town",jp:"でんでんタウン",city:"Osaka",category:"Entertainment",tier:3,desc:"Osaka's Akihabara: anime, retro games, electronics, and maid cafés along Nipponbashi. Smaller than Akiba but calmer, cheaper, and you can actually browse without elbows.",hours:"~10:00–19:00",cost:"Free entry",station:"Ebisucho / Nipponbashi",duration:"1–2h",decNote:"Often cheaper than Akihabara for identical retro carts — buy here, skip the Tokyo markup."},{id:"spa-world",name:"Spa World",jp:"スパワールド",city:"Osaka",category:"Entertainment",tier:3,desc:"A theme park of baths: European floor (Roman, Finnish sauna) and Asian floor (hinoki, outdoor rock baths) that swap by gender monthly. Cheap, open basically always, and a legit group recovery day.",hours:"10:00–8:45 next morning",cost:"~$9 (¥1,500)",station:"Dobutsuen-mae (direct)",duration:"2–4h",decNote:"The outdoor baths against December cold air are the whole point. Tattoo rules vary by area — check current policy; small covered tattoos often tolerated."},{id:"hep-five",name:"HEP FIVE Ferris Wheel",jp:"HEP FIVE観覧車",city:"Osaka",category:"Viewpoint",tier:3,desc:"A giant red Ferris wheel bolted to the ROOF of an Umeda shopping mall, because Osaka. Fifteen minutes per rotation, 106m at the top, a whale sculpture in the atrium below for reasons nobody questions.",hours:"11:00–22:45 (last ride)",cost:"$5 (¥800), covered by Osaka Amazing Pass",station:"Umeda/Osaka (5 min)",duration:"30–45 min",decNote:"Night ride over the Umeda winter lights; gondolas seat 4 — fly a two-car formation."},{id:"minoo",name:"Minoo Park & Waterfall",jp:"箕面公園",city:"Osaka",category:"Park & Nature",tier:3,desc:"A 2.8km gorge trail from the station to a 33m waterfall — Osaka's fastest exit into real nature, 30 minutes from Umeda. Momiji tempura (battered, deep-fried maple leaves) sold along the path is real, ancient, and weirdly good.",hours:"Trail 24h; shops daytime",cost:"Free",station:"Minoo (Hankyu, via Ishibashi-handai-mae)",duration:"Half day",decNote:"Foliage is done by late Dec — what's left is a silent trail, a cold clear waterfall, and zero people. Monkeys exist; don't feed."},{id:"namba-yasaka",name:"Namba Yasaka Shrine",jp:"難波八阪神社",city:"Osaka",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:3,desc:"A 12-meter lion head, mid-roar, with a stage for a mouth — it swallows evil spirits and grants victory, so students and salarymen line up before big days. The best 20-minute detour in the Namba neighborhood, full stop.",hours:"6:30–17:00",cost:"Free",station:"Namba (6 min walk)",duration:"20–30 min",decNote:"Morning-of-checkout move on Dec 29: victory blessing, then the airport. The group photo in the mouth's shadow is mandatory."},{id:"sumiyoshi",name:"Sumiyoshi Taisha",jp:"住吉大社",city:"Osaka",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:2,desc:"One of Japan's oldest shrines (founded 211 AD, before Buddhism even landed), built in a pure native style you won't see in Kyoto: straight roofs, crossed finials, and the steep vermillion Sorihashi drum bridge. Ride the rattling Hankai tram — Osaka's last streetcar — to get there.",hours:"6:30–17:00 (Dec)",cost:"Free",station:"Sumiyoshitaisha (Nankai, ~10 min from Namba)",duration:"1–1.5h",decNote:"Days before we leave, they're rigging for 2 million+ hatsumode visitors — banners, stalls, pre-New-Year electricity."},{id:"nara-park",name:"Nara Park Deer",jp:"奈良公園",city:"Nara",category:"Park & Nature",tier:1,desc:"1,200 sacred sika deer who have learned to BOW for crackers (shika senbei). They are polite right up until they smell paper in your pocket. Protect your maps, your tickets, and your dignity.",hours:"24h (deer on duty all day)",cost:"Free; crackers ~$1 (¥200)",station:"Kintetsu-Nara (5 min walk)",duration:"All morning, between sights",decNote:"Winter coats make them extra round. Fewer tourists = more deer attention per person — pockets get frisked harder."},{id:"todaiji",name:"Todai-ji & the Great Buddha",jp:"東大寺",city:"Nara",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:1,desc:"A 15-meter, 500-ton bronze Buddha from 752 AD inside one of the largest wooden buildings on Earth. A pillar inside has a hole the size of the Buddha's nostril — crawl through for enlightenment (kids and limber adults only).",hours:"8:00–17:00 (winter)",cost:"$5 (¥800)",station:"Bus/walk through Nara Park",duration:"1–1.5h",decNote:"The vast hall is unheated — it's basically the temperature of outside, so the December crowd is thin and the photos are clean."},{id:"kasuga",name:"Kasuga Taisha",jp:"春日大社",city:"Nara",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:2,desc:"Shinto shrine at the forest's edge, famous for 3,000 lanterns — stone ones lining the mossy approach, bronze ones hanging in golden rows. Deer wander the paths like they own them (they do).",hours:"6:30–17:00 (winter)",cost:"Grounds free; inner $4 (¥700)",station:"Walk from Todai-ji (15 min)",duration:"1h",decNote:"The lantern-lined forest approach is at its most atmospheric on a frosty, mist-hung December morning."},{id:"nakatanidou",name:"Nakatanidou Mochi Pounding",jp:"中谷堂",city:"Nara",category:"Market & Shopping",tier:2,desc:"The viral high-speed mochi-pounding shop. Two men hammer molten rice dough in perfect rhythm, then sell you the result — warm yomogi mochi dusted in kinako. Best dollar in Nara.",hours:"10:00–19:00; pounding sporadically",cost:"~$1 (¥200)/mochi",station:"Kintetsu-Nara (3 min)",duration:"20 min",decNote:"A scalding-fresh mochi straight off the mallet is exactly what a near-freezing Nara morning calls for."},{id:"naramachi",name:"Naramachi Old Quarter",jp:"ならまち",city:"Nara",category:"Landmark",tier:3,desc:"Preserved merchant district of lattice-front machiya houses, now cafés, sake breweries, and craft shops. Harushika brewery's tasting (5 sakes) is the local move. The quiet half-hour between temple stops.",hours:"Shops ~10:00–17:00",cost:"Free to wander; sake tasting ~$3 (¥500)",station:"Kintetsu-Nara (10 min)",duration:"1h",decNote:"Warm sake tasting at Harushika is a justified December detour after the cold temple circuit."},{id:"kofukuji",name:"Kofuku-ji & Five-Story Pagoda",jp:"興福寺",city:"Nara",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:2,desc:"Nara's other giant: a 50-meter five-story pagoda (Japan's second-tallest, freshly restored) and the National Treasure Museum housing the three-faced, six-armed Ashura statue that Japan genuinely fan-girls over. Right where you enter the park from Kintetsu-Nara.",hours:"9:00–17:00",cost:"Grounds free; Treasure Museum $5 (¥700)",station:"Kintetsu-Nara (5 min)",duration:"45 min–1h",decNote:"First stop off the train into Nara Park — knock it out before Todai-ji, the deer start working you immediately."},{id:"peace-park",name:"Peace Memorial Park & Museum",jp:"平和記念公園",city:"Hiroshima",category:"Museum & Art",tier:1,desc:"The museum is devastating and essential — personal artifacts, survivor testimony, no flinching. Outside: the skeletal A-Bomb Dome, the Children's Peace Monument draped in paper cranes, and the eternal flame. Allow real time and emotional bandwidth.",hours:"Museum 8:30–18:00 (Dec–Feb)",cost:"$1 (¥200) museum",station:"Genbaku Dome-mae (tram)",duration:"2.5–3h",decNote:"Go first thing — quiet, cold morning light, then lift spirits with okonomiyaki and the Miyajima ferry after."},{id:"itsukushima",name:"Itsukushima Floating Torii",jp:"厳島神社",city:"Hiroshima",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:1,desc:"The vermillion gate rising from the sea — one of Japan's three official 'best views' since 1643. At high tide it floats; at low tide you walk out and touch it. The shrine itself stands on stilts over the water.",hours:"Shrine 6:30–17:00 (Dec)",cost:"$3 (¥500) shrine; ferry ~$1 (¥200)",station:"Miyajimaguchi + 10-min ferry",duration:"Half day (island)",decNote:"Check the tide table and aim to see BOTH states. Oyster stalls peak in December — grilled Miyajima oysters are the reason to come hungry."},{id:"misen",name:"Mt. Misen Ropeway",jp:"弥山",city:"Hiroshima",category:"Viewpoint",tier:2,desc:"Miyajima's sacred peak via two ropeways + a 30-min summit scramble: the Seto Inland Sea scattered with islands below, and a temple fire that's burned for 1,200 years (it lit Hiroshima's eternal flame).",hours:"9:00–16:00 (winter)",cost:"$13 (¥2,000) round trip",station:"Momijidani station (island)",duration:"2–2.5h",decNote:"December air gives the clearest Inland Sea views of the year. Wind at the summit is brutal — bring the windproof layer."},{id:"okonomimura",name:"Okonomimura",jp:"お好み村",city:"Hiroshima",category:"Market & Shopping",tier:2,desc:"Three floors, 20+ griddle counters, one religion: Hiroshima-style okonomiyaki layered with yakisoba noodles. Sit at any counter, watch the spatula work, take a side in the eternal Osaka-vs-Hiroshima war.",hours:"~11:00–22:00 (stalls vary)",cost:"$6–9 (¥1,000–1,500)",station:"Hatchobori (tram)",duration:"1h",decNote:"Hot griddle, cold street — the ideal Hiroshima lunch between the Peace Park and the Miyajima ferry."},{id:"shukkeien",name:"Shukkei-en Garden",jp:"縮景園",city:"Hiroshima",category:"Park & Nature",tier:3,desc:"A 1620 daimyo garden of miniaturized landscapes — arched bridges, koi, tea pavilions ringing a central pond — rebuilt after 1945 like everything else here. The gentle palate-cleanser between the Peace Museum and okonomiyaki.",hours:"9:00–17:00 (Dec)",cost:"$2 (¥260)",station:"Shukkeien-mae (tram)",duration:"45 min–1h",decNote:"Winter-pruned pines, no crowds — the quiet exhale the Hiroshima day needs before the ferry to Miyajima."},{id:"hiroshima-castle",name:"Hiroshima Castle",jp:"広島城",city:"Hiroshima",category:"Landmark",tier:3,desc:"The 'Carp Castle' — a 1590s fortress flattened by the bomb and faithfully rebuilt in 1958, with a wooden interior museum on Hiroshima's samurai era. Wide moat, a reconstructed gate and turret, and a shrine in the grounds.",hours:"9:00–17:00 (winter, last entry 16:30)",cost:"$3 (¥400) keep",station:"Genbaku Dome-mae + 10 min walk",duration:"1h",decNote:"A short, warm-ish indoor stop a few minutes from the Peace Park if the cold morning runs ahead of schedule."},{id:"kamakura-daibutsu",name:"Kamakura Great Buddha (Kotoku-in)",jp:"鎌倉大仏・高徳院",city:"Day Trips",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:1,desc:"The 13.4m, 121-ton open-air bronze Buddha cast in 1252 at Kotoku-in — its hall washed away by a tsunami centuries ago, so it has sat under open sky ever since. You can step INSIDE the hollow statue for a few yen. The anchor of the Dec 19 day trip.",hours:"8:00–17:00 (winter); interior till 16:30",cost:"$3 (¥500) + interior $0.30 (¥50)",station:"Hase (Enoden), 7 min from Kamakura",duration:"1–1.5h",decNote:"Crisp December air, low crowds, and a clear blue sky behind the bronze — the Dec 19 Kamakura/Enoshima centerpiece."},{id:"hase-dera",name:"Hase-dera Temple",jp:"長谷寺",city:"Day Trips",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:2,desc:"Hillside temple a few minutes from the Great Buddha: a 9m gilded eleven-headed Kannon, a cave of carved deities, ranks of tiny Jizo statues, and a terrace with the best ocean view in Kamakura over Sagami Bay.",hours:"8:00–16:30 (winter)",cost:"$3 (¥400)",station:"Hase (Enoden)",duration:"1–1.5h",decNote:"Clear December afternoons give you Sagami Bay and, on the best days, Mt. Fuji from the terrace. Pairs with the Great Buddha next door."},{id:"tsurugaoka",name:"Tsurugaoka Hachimangu",jp:"鶴岡八幡宮",city:"Day Trips",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:2,desc:"Kamakura's grand shrine at the top of the Wakamiya-oji approach, founded by the Minamoto shoguns in 1063. A broad stone-and-vermillion stairway, an arched drum bridge, and the lantern-lined Komachi-dori snack street running back toward the station.",hours:"Grounds ~6:00–20:00 (Dec)",cost:"Free (treasure hall extra)",station:"Kamakura (10 min walk)",duration:"1h",decNote:"Late December it's draped for hatsumode — stalls going up, banners out. Komachi-dori is the warm-snack engine of the day trip."},{id:"enoshima",name:"Enoshima Island",jp:"江の島",city:"Day Trips",category:"Park & Nature",tier:2,desc:"Little island off the Shonan coast linked by causeway: shrine caves, a lighthouse-garden combo (the Sea Candle), and the best Fuji-over-the-ocean sunset near Tokyo. Ride the retro Enoden tram from Kamakura along the coast to get here.",hours:"Island always; Sea Candle till ~20:00",cost:"~$7 (¥1,100) island/escalator/Sea Candle pass",station:"Katase-Enoshima (Odakyu) / Enoshima (Enoden)",duration:"Half day",decNote:"The winter Sea Candle illumination ('Shonan no Hoseki') is shockingly good, and December gives the clearest Fuji-over-Sagami-Bay sunset of the year. The natural finale of the Dec 19 trip."},{id:"enoden",name:"Enoden Coastal Tram",jp:"江ノ電",city:"Day Trips",category:"Landmark",tier:2,desc:"The century-old single-track tram that threads from Kamakura to Enoshima, squeezing between houses and then bursting out along the sea wall at Kamakurakokomae — the level crossing made famous by a certain basketball anime opening. Pay by Suica, just tap on.",hours:"Trains ~6:00–23:00",cost:"~$2 (¥260) per hop, or $5 (¥800) day pass",station:"Kamakura ⇄ Fujisawa line",duration:"Built into the day",decNote:"The Kamakurakokomae crossing with Fuji across the winter sea is the photo. Tap your Suica — no JR Pass needed, no special ticket."},{id:"himeji",name:"Himeji Castle",jp:"姫路城",city:"Day Trips",category:"Landmark",tier:2,desc:"Japan's greatest surviving original castle — the White Heron, never burned, never bombed, never rebuilt in concrete. Six floors of steep ladder-stairs inside the 1609 keep. A quick SmartEX shinkansen hop past Kobe.",hours:"9:00–16:00 (in by)",cost:"$6 (¥1,000); +$3 (¥520) with Koko-en garden",station:"Himeji (15 min walk)",duration:"Half day",decNote:"Winter white walls against a clear blue December sky — bring sunglasses, seriously. Book the shinkansen leg on SmartEX."},{id:"kobe",name:"Kobe — Beef & Harborland",jp:"神戸",city:"Day Trips",category:"Landmark",tier:3,desc:"20 minutes from Osaka: eat actual certified Kobe beef in its hometown (teppanyaki lunch sets are half the dinner price), then walk the harbor at dusk. The Kitano district's old foreign mansions add a European detour.",hours:"Varies",cost:"Kobe beef lunch $38–94 (¥6,000–15,000)",station:"Sannomiya (JR/Hankyu)",duration:"Half day",decNote:"The Kobe Luminarie illumination — the city's memorial light festival — runs in December; check exact 2026 dates and time the harbor walk after the ~16:50 sunset."},{id:"arima",name:"Arima Onsen",jp:"有馬温泉",city:"Day Trips",category:"Entertainment",tier:3,desc:"Japan's oldest hot-spring town, hidden behind Mt. Rokko 30 minutes from Kobe: rust-gold 'kinsen' iron water at Kin no Yu, clear 'ginsen' baths at Gin no Yu, and a walkable old town of steamy lanes, sake shops, and carbonated-spring crackers.",hours:"Kin no Yu ~8:00–22:00",cost:"$4–5 (¥650–850)/bath; two-bath pass available",station:"Arima-Onsen (Kobe Electric Rly / bus from Sannomiya)",duration:"Half day",decNote:"Bolt it onto the Himeji/Kobe day: castle morning, beef lunch, Arima soak, harbor lights at night. December steam against the cold is the whole point."},{id:"nikko",name:"Nikko Toshogu",jp:"日光東照宮",city:"Day Trips",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:3,desc:"Tokugawa Ieyasu's mausoleum — the most extravagantly carved, gilded shrine complex in Japan, in a cedar forest two hours north of Tokyo. Home of the original see-no-evil monkeys and the sleeping cat.",hours:"9:00–16:00 (winter)",cost:"$10 (¥1,600)",station:"Tobu-Nikko (~2h from Asakusa)",duration:"Full day trip",decNote:"Often dusted with snow in late December — stunning, but pack the warm layer and check Tobu line winter timetables."},{id:"yokohama",name:"Yokohama — Minato Mirai & Ramen Museum",jp:"横浜",city:"Day Trips",category:"Entertainment",tier:3,desc:"30 minutes from Shibuya: a harbor skyline, Japan's biggest Chinatown, the Cup Noodles Museum (build your own flavor), and the Shin-Yokohama Ramen Museum — regional ramen shops in a recreated 1958 retro streetscape.",hours:"Varies",cost:"Ramen Museum $4 (¥600) + bowls",station:"Minatomirai / Shin-Yokohama",duration:"Half–full day",decNote:"Yokohama's harbor illumination + the Red Brick Warehouse Christmas Market (glühwein, German stalls) is the big December draw."},{id:"kagurazaka",name:"Kagurazaka",jp:"神楽坂",city:"Tokyo",category:"Landmark",tier:3,desc:"Tokyo's 'little Paris' on a hill — a former geisha quarter of cobbled side-alleys, hidden ryotei, French bistros, and stone-paved slopes that feel nothing like the rest of the city. The grown-up, low-key night out when the crew wants a break from neon.",hours:"Shops/bars ~11:00–23:00",cost:"Free to wander",station:"Iidabashi / Kagurazaka (Tozai line)",duration:"1.5–2h",decNote:"The narrow back-lanes glow with lantern light after the ~16:30 sunset — duck into a tiny izakaya or a French wine bar to thaw out."},{id:"sunshine-city",name:"Sunshine City, Ikebukuro",jp:"サンシャインシティ",city:"Tokyo",category:"Entertainment",tier:3,desc:"A self-contained indoor city in Ikebukuro: aquarium on the roof, planetarium, Pokémon Center Mega Tokyo, Namja Town indoor theme park, and a 240m observatory. The all-weather group bunker when December turns nasty.",hours:"Malls 10:00–20:00; attractions vary",cost:"Free entry; aquarium $16 (¥2,600)",station:"Higashi-Ikebukuro (Yurakucho) / Ikebukuro",duration:"Half day",decNote:"Everything is indoors and warm — the obvious move for the coldest, greyest afternoon of the trip."},{id:"koenji",name:"Koenji Vintage District",jp:"高円寺",city:"Tokyo",category:"Market & Shopping",tier:3,desc:"Tokyo's punk-and-vintage heartland: dozens of secondhand and used-clothing shops, dive bars, curry counters, and record stores packed around the station. Grittier and cheaper than Shimokitazawa, with the best thrift racks in the city.",hours:"Shops 12:00–20:00",cost:"Free to browse",station:"Koenji (JR Chuo)",duration:"2–3h",decNote:"Winter coats and leather jackets fill the vintage racks in December — prime hunting season. Warm up in a tiny gado-shita bar under the tracks after."},{id:"ryogoku-sumo",name:"Ryogoku Kokugikan & Sumo Town",jp:"両国国技館",city:"Tokyo",category:"Landmark",tier:2,desc:"The national sumo arena and the neighborhood built around it: stable practices, chanko-nabe (sumo hot-pot) restaurants run by ex-wrestlers, and the Sumo Museum inside the hall. Hand-print plaques of grand champions line the streets.",hours:"Museum 10:00–16:30, closed weekends; restaurants midday + evening",cost:"Museum free; chanko lunch ~$13 (¥2,000)",station:"Ryogoku (JR Sobu / Oedo line)",duration:"1.5–2h",decNote:"No December tournament (the year's last is in Fukuoka), but a steaming pot of chanko-nabe shared 8 ways is exactly the right call on a freezing day."},{id:"red-tokyo-tower",name:"RED° Tokyo Tower",jp:"RED° TOKYO TOWER",city:"Tokyo",category:"Entertainment",tier:3,desc:"Japan's biggest e-sports and digital-entertainment park, stacked across the base floors of Tokyo Tower: racing sims, fighting-game booths, VR, AR sports, and rhythm games. A pay-one-price playground built for a competitive crew of 8.",hours:"10:00–22:00",cost:"Day pass ~$22 (¥3,500)",station:"Akabanebashi (Oedo line)",duration:"2–3h",decNote:"All indoors and warm, right under the winter-lit Tokyo Tower — settle the trip's rivalries on the racing sims, then shoot the tower light-up outside."},{id:"yurakucho-gado",name:"Yurakucho Gado-shita Izakaya",jp:"有楽町ガード下",city:"Tokyo",category:"Nightlife",tier:3,desc:"The arches directly under the JR tracks between Yurakucho and Shimbashi, packed with smoky izakaya and yakitori counters where salarymen drink after work. Trains rumble overhead while you grill skewers and clink mugs — peak old-Tokyo night out, ten minutes from Ginza's glitz.",hours:"~17:00–24:00",cost:"$19–31 (¥3,000–5,000) per person",station:"Yurakucho (JR Yamanote) / Shimbashi",duration:"2–3h",decNote:"Some counters fire up outdoor charcoal grills and hang plastic curtains against the cold — beer, smoke, and a December chill is the whole salaryman ritual. Great for a loud table of 8."},{id:"toji",name:"To-ji Temple & Five-Story Pagoda",jp:"東寺",city:"Kyoto",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:2,desc:"Japan's tallest wooden pagoda (57m), the symbol of Kyoto, five minutes from the station. The Lecture Hall holds a 3-D mandala of 21 Heian-era Buddhist statues arranged in space — one of the most powerful rooms in the city.",hours:"8:00–17:00",cost:"$8 (¥1,000) when halls open; grounds free",station:"Toji (Kintetsu) / Kyoto Stn (15 min walk)",duration:"1–1.5h",decNote:"The Kobo-ichi flea market fills the grounds on the 21st (Dec 21) with 1,000+ stalls of antiques, food, and junk — the year's biggest. Time the Kyoto leg around it if you can."},{id:"kyoto-railway",name:"Kyoto Railway Museum",jp:"京都鉄道博物館",city:"Kyoto",category:"Museum & Art",tier:2,desc:"53 real trains from steam locomotives to the first-generation shinkansen, a working roundhouse and turntable, a vast operating diorama, and a train simulator. You can ride a genuine steam train on the grounds. Unapologetically fun for grown men.",hours:"10:00–17:00, closed Wed",cost:"$8 (¥1,500)",station:"Umekoji-Kyotonishi (Sagano line) / bus from Kyoto Stn",duration:"2–3h",decNote:"Huge, indoors, and heated — the rainy/freezing-day card in Kyoto. The steam-train ride puffs satisfyingly in the cold air."},{id:"manga-museum",name:"Kyoto International Manga Museum",jp:"京都国際マンガミュージアム",city:"Kyoto",category:"Museum & Art",tier:3,desc:"A converted elementary school holding 300,000 manga volumes on a 200m 'Wall of Manga' — pull anything off the shelf and read it anywhere, including on the lawn. Part museum, part the world's coziest library.",hours:"10:30–17:30, closed Tue/Wed",cost:"$6 (¥900)",station:"Karasuma-Oike (subway)",duration:"1.5–2h",decNote:"Grab a stack, claim a warm corner, and wait out a cold Kyoto afternoon. English manga shelf exists for the non-readers."},{id:"tempozan-wheel",name:"Tempozan Ferris Wheel & Harbor",jp:"天保山大観覧車",city:"Osaka",category:"Viewpoint",tier:3,desc:"One of the world's tallest Ferris wheels (112m) on the bay beside Kaiyukan aquarium, with see-through gondolas and a night-color light show on the wheel itself. The Legoland Discovery Center and a retro food market sit at its base.",hours:"10:00–22:00 (last ride 21:30)",cost:"$6 (¥900); gondolas seat 6–8",station:"Osakako (Chuo line)",duration:"45 min–1h",decNote:"Stack it onto the Kaiyukan visit — night ride over the harbor's winter illumination, the whole crew in one or two gondolas."},{id:"nakanoshima",name:"Nakanoshima",jp:"中之島",city:"Osaka",category:"Landmark",tier:3,desc:"The cultured sandbar between Osaka's two rivers: the red-brick Meiji Central Public Hall, the Museum of Oriental Ceramics, the Nakanoshima Museum of Art, and riverside promenades. Osaka's calm, handsome counterweight to Dotonbori's chaos.",hours:"Park 24h; museums ~10:00–17:00, many closed Mon",cost:"Park free; museums $8–13 (¥1,200–2,000)",station:"Naniwabashi / Yodoyabashi",duration:"1.5–2h",decNote:"The 'OSAKA光のルネサンス' (Festival of the Lights) wraps the river island and the Public Hall in projection-mapping and illumination all December — Osaka's flagship winter light event."},{id:"wakakusa",name:"Mt. Wakakusa",jp:"若草山",city:"Nara",category:"Viewpoint",tier:3,desc:"A grassy, treeless hill rising straight out of Nara Park — a 30–40 min walk up grazed slopes (more deer up top) to a summit panorama over the whole Nara basin and the temple roofs below. The best free view in the city.",hours:"9:00–17:00 (open mid-Mar to mid-Dec)",cost:"$1 (¥150)",station:"Walk from Todai-ji / Kasuga area",duration:"1.5–2h",decNote:"Closes for winter in mid-December (and the famous Yamayaki grass-burning is in January) — check the gate is still open for our dates before committing the climb."},{id:"nara-national-museum",name:"Nara National Museum",jp:"奈良国立博物館",city:"Nara",category:"Museum & Art",tier:3,desc:"Japan's premier collection of Buddhist art — bronzes, sculpture, and painting — in an elegant 1894 hall inside Nara Park. The annual Shoso-in exhibition of the emperors' 1,300-year-old treasures runs in late autumn; the standing collection is superb year-round.",hours:"9:30–17:00, closed Mon",cost:"$5 (¥700)",station:"Kintetsu-Nara (15 min walk)",duration:"1–1.5h",decNote:"A warm, quiet refuge in the middle of the park when the December cold off the temple stones gets to you — and the deer can't follow you in."},{id:"mazda-museum",name:"Mazda Museum",jp:"マツダミュージアム",city:"Hiroshima",category:"Museum & Art",tier:3,desc:"A free factory tour at Mazda's Hiroshima HQ: the rotary-engine history, classic cars and Le Mans racers, and a look down onto a live assembly line where finished cars roll off. Reservation-only, in English, and catnip for any gearhead in the group.",hours:"Tours by reservation, weekdays, closed weekends/holidays",cost:"Free (book ahead online)",station:"Mukainada (JR Sanyo line) + shuttle/walk",duration:"1.5h",decNote:"Indoors and free — book the English slot weeks ahead. A solid morning before the Peace Park if the car guys outvote the rest."},{id:"kawaguchiko",name:"Lake Kawaguchiko & Mt. Fuji",jp:"河口湖・富士山",city:"Day Trips",category:"Park & Nature",tier:2,desc:"The classic head-on Mt. Fuji view across a lake in the Fuji Five Lakes region: the Chureito Pagoda shot (pagoda + Fuji), the Kachi Kachi ropeway, lakeside onsen ryokan, and the Fujiyoshida retro shopping street where Fuji fills the end of the road.",hours:"Ropeway 9:00–17:00; sights vary",cost:"Express bus from Shinjuku ~$11 (¥1,800) each way; ropeway $6 (¥1,000)",station:"Kawaguchiko (bus from Shinjuku, ~2h)",duration:"Full day trip",decNote:"December is the single best month for a clear, snow-capped Fuji — bone-dry air, no summer haze. Take the highway bus from Shinjuku; pack serious layers, the lake wind is vicious."},{id:"koyasan",name:"Koyasan (Mt. Koya)",jp:"高野山",city:"Day Trips",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:3,desc:"A 1,200-year-old Buddhist monastery town atop a sacred mountain south of Osaka, reached by a cliff-hugging cablecar. Walk the Okunoin cemetery — 200,000 moss-covered graves under towering cedars — to the mausoleum of Kobo Daishi, then stay in a temple lodging (shukubo) for monk-cooked vegetarian dinner and dawn prayers.",hours:"Okunoin 24h; halls ~8:30–17:00",cost:"Train+cablecar from Osaka ~$11 (¥1,700) each way; temple stay $63+ (¥10,000+)",station:"Koyasan (Nankai Koya line + cablecar, ~2h from Namba)",duration:"Full day or overnight",decNote:"Brutally cold and often snow-covered in December — which makes the cedar-shadowed Okunoin one of the most atmospheric places in Japan. An overnight temple stay is the move; bring thermals, the lodgings are barely heated."},{id:"kurama",name:"Kurama & Kibune Onsen",jp:"鞍馬・貴船",city:"Day Trips",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:3,desc:"A mountain village north of Kyoto on the retro Eizan railway: hike the cedar trail over the pass from Kurama-dera temple down to Kibune shrine, then soak at Kurama Onsen's open-air rotenburo facing the forested slope. The legendary training ground of the tengu mountain goblins.",hours:"Onsen ~10:00–20:00; temple 9:00–16:15",cost:"Eizan train from Kyoto ~$3 (¥470); rotenburo ~$8 (¥1,300)",station:"Kurama (Eizan line, ~30 min from Demachiyanagi)",duration:"Half–full day",decNote:"The open-air bath surrounded by snow-dusted cedars is the entire reason to come in December. Check the Eizan line winter timetable — service thins out, and the trail can ice over."},{id:"nakano-broadway",name:"Nakano Broadway",jp:"中野ブロードウェイ",city:"Tokyo",category:"Market & Shopping",tier:3,desc:"A 1966 retro mall that became the holy site of otaku collecting: floor after floor of Mandarake outlets stuffed with vintage figures, manga, cels, watches, and the genuinely rare stuff Akihabara sold off years ago. Ground-floor Daily Chiko serves an absurd eight-flavor soft-serve tower.",hours:"Shops ~12:00–20:00",cost:"Free entry; wallet at own risk",station:"Nakano (JR Chuo, 5 min walk)",duration:"2–3h",decNote:"Fully indoors and quirky — a great warm rabbit-hole on a cold afternoon, calmer and often cheaper than Akihabara for the same treasures."},{id:"hokusai-museum",name:"Sumida Hokusai Museum",jp:"すみだ北斎美術館",city:"Tokyo",category:"Museum & Art",tier:3,desc:"A sleek mirrored-aluminum museum dedicated to Katsushika Hokusai — the 'Great Wave' artist who lived his whole life in this Sumida neighborhood. Compact, high-quality, with touchscreen deep-dives and a life-size recreation of his cluttered studio.",hours:"9:30–17:30, closed Mon",cost:"$3 (¥400) permanent collection",station:"Ryogoku (JR Sobu / Oedo line)",duration:"1–1.5h",decNote:"Pair it with sumo-town Ryogoku and a chanko-nabe lunch — a tidy, heated half-day on a freezing east-side morning."},{id:"kennin-ji",name:"Kennin-ji Temple",jp:"建仁寺",city:"Kyoto",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:3,desc:"Kyoto's oldest Zen temple (1202), tucked right behind Gion yet almost crowd-free. The draw is the ceiling: a colossal twin-dragon painting glaring down over the Dharma Hall, plus the famous gold-leaf 'Wind and Thunder Gods' screen and serene rock gardens you can sit and stare at.",hours:"10:00–16:30",cost:"$4 (¥600)",station:"Gion-Shijo (Keihan) / Higashiyama",duration:"45 min–1h",decNote:"The quiet, warm-indoors counterpoint to a Gion evening — sit on the tatami facing the rock garden while December empties the place out."},{id:"nishijin-orinasukan",name:"Nishijin Textile District & Orinasukan",jp:"西陣・織成舘",city:"Kyoto",category:"Museum & Art",tier:3,desc:"Kyoto's thousand-year-old weaving quarter, home of Nishijin-ori brocade. The Orinasukan museum (in a former kimono workshop) shows hand-loom weavers at work, while the nearby Nishijin Textile Center runs free kimono shows and lets you try a hand loom.",hours:"Orinasukan 10:00–16:00, closed Mon; Textile Center 10:00–16:00",cost:"Orinasukan $4 (¥500); Textile Center free",station:"Imadegawa (Karasuma line) + walk / bus",duration:"1–1.5h",decNote:"An indoor, hands-on hour of real Kyoto craft a short walk from the Imperial Palace — warm refuge with a souvenir angle when the December streets bite."},{id:"kawagoe",name:"Kawagoe (Little Edo)",jp:"川越",city:"Day Trips",category:"Landmark",tier:3,desc:"A preserved Edo-era merchant town 30 minutes from Ikebukuro: a street of black-walled kurazukuri warehouses, the 'Bell of Time' wooden tower, a candy alley (Kashiya Yokocho), and Kawagoe's famous sweet-potato everything. The closest thing to time travel near Tokyo.",hours:"Shops ~10:00–17:00",cost:"Tobu/Seibu train ~$3 (¥500) each way; free to wander",station:"Kawagoe / Kawagoeshi (Tobu/Seibu, ~30 min)",duration:"Half day",decNote:"Half the day-trip travel of Nikko or Kawaguchiko. The old streets and Hikawa Shrine dress up for year-end; warm roasted sweet potato is the December street snack."},{id:"takao",name:"Mt. Takao",jp:"高尾山",city:"Day Trips",category:"Park & Nature",tier:3,desc:"Tokyo's most-climbed mountain, 50 minutes from Shinjuku: a cablecar or a forest trail to a 599m summit with a Tengu-guarded temple (Yakuo-in) halfway up, monkey park, and — on a clear day — a clean Mt. Fuji view from the top. A genuine half-day hike inside the city limits.",hours:"Trails daylight; cablecar ~8:00–17:30",cost:"Keio train ~$3 (¥430) each way; cablecar $6 (¥1,000) round trip",station:"Takaosanguchi (Keio line)",duration:"Half day",decNote:"December's dry air gives the year's best Fuji view from the summit (a Diamond Fuji window falls around the solstice). Trails can ice — wear grippy shoes, pack a warm layer, reward the climb with the famous tororo-soba at the base."},{id:"miyashita-park",name:"Miyashita Park & Shibuya Yokocho",jp:"ミヤシタパーク・渋谷横丁",city:"Tokyo",category:"Nightlife",tier:2,desc:"A rooftop park bolted on top of a sleek Shibuya mall, with a skate park and bouldering wall up top — and on the ground floor, Shibuya Yokocho: a single neon alley where 19 stalls serve the regional food of all 47 prefectures, open till the small hours. Oden from Aomori, Okinawan awamori, Kyushu motsunabe, all in one corridor that roars till 5am.",hours:"Yokocho ~11:00–5:00; rooftop park 8:00–23:00",cost:"Free entry; eat/drink $19–31 (¥3,000–5,000)",station:"Shibuya (3 min walk)",duration:"2–3h",decNote:"The covered, heated alley is a loud, easy landing pad for 8 on a freezing Shibuya night — grab a long table, order across prefectures, and let the December cold stay outside."},{id:"edo-tokyo-openair",name:"Edo-Tokyo Open Air Architectural Museum",jp:"江戸東京たてもの園",city:"Tokyo",category:"Museum & Art",tier:3,desc:"An open-air park in Koganei where 30 real historic buildings were relocated and rebuilt — a thatched farmhouse, a Meiji photo studio, a 1920s shitamachi shopping street, and the bathhouse and stationery shop that Miyazaki openly used as references for 'Spirited Away.' You walk inside every one of them.",hours:"9:30–16:30 (winter), closed Mon",cost:"$3 (¥400)",station:"Musashi-Koganei (JR Chuo) + bus",duration:"2–3h",decNote:"The old houses light their irori hearths and braziers in winter for atmosphere (and warmth) — the Ghibli pilgrimage half of the group will love it, and it's a calm pairing with the nearby Ghibli Museum in Mitaka."},{id:"shimokita-bonus",name:"Bonus Track & Shimokitazawa Backstreets",jp:"ボーナストラック",city:"Tokyo",category:"Entertainment",tier:3,desc:"A purpose-built micro-village of indie shops, a soy-sauce-ice-cream stand, a craft beer bar, a bookshop, and a fermented-foods specialist, all wrapped around a central courtyard a few minutes from Shimokitazawa station. The newest, most deliberate slice of Tokyo's most charming neighborhood.",hours:"Shops ~11:00–20:00; bars later",cost:"Free to wander",station:"Shimokitazawa (Odakyu/Keio)",duration:"1–2h",decNote:"The courtyard strings up holiday lights and the bars set out heaters — a warm, low-key evening hang that pairs with the wider Shimokitazawa vintage crawl. Eight minutes from Shibuya."},{id:"tokyo-national-museum",name:"Tokyo National Museum",jp:"東京国立博物館",city:"Tokyo",category:"Museum & Art",tier:2,desc:"Japan's oldest and largest museum, anchoring Ueno Park: samurai armor and swords, Buddhist sculpture, ukiyo-e, and national treasures across multiple halls — the Honkan (Japanese gallery) alone is a self-contained masterclass, and the Gallery of Horyu-ji Treasures is a serene modern-architecture bonus.",hours:"9:30–17:00, closed Mon",cost:"$6 (¥1,000) regular collection",station:"Ueno (JR Yamanote) / Uguisudani",duration:"2–3h",decNote:"The big warm indoor anchor of a freezing Ueno day — do the sword and armor rooms in the Honkan even if you do nothing else, then thaw out with Ameyoko crab-shopping chaos next door."},{id:"ryoanji",name:"Ryoan-ji Rock Garden",jp:"龍安寺",city:"Kyoto",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:2,desc:"The most famous Zen rock garden on Earth: 15 stones in raked white gravel, arranged so you can never see all 15 at once from any angle — nobody fully agrees what it means, which is the point. A 20-minute walk or short bus from Kinkaku-ji, with a mossy pond garden as the warm-up.",hours:"8:30–16:30 (winter)",cost:"$4 (¥600)",station:"Bus to Ryoanji-mae / Randen Ryoanji",duration:"45 min–1h",decNote:"Sit on the wooden veranda in the cold quiet and actually look — December empties out the gravel-garden crowd that ruins it in spring. Stack it with Kinkaku-ji, a short hop north."},{id:"pontocho",name:"Pontocho Alley",jp:"先斗町",city:"Kyoto",category:"Nightlife",tier:1,desc:"Kyoto's most atmospheric dining lane: a single lantern-lit alley barely wide enough for two, packed with everything from cheap yakitori counters to centuries-old kaiseki houses, running between Shijo and Sanjo along the Kamogawa river. Look for the plover-bird crest on the lanterns — that's the Pontocho mark.",hours:"Most places ~17:00–23:00",cost:"$19–63 (¥3,000–10,000) depending on the door you pick",station:"Gion-Shijo (Keihan) / Kawaramachi (Hankyu)",duration:"1.5–2.5h",decNote:"The lantern-glow alley on a cold, dry December night is peak Kyoto — book a riverside-window table if you can. Splits naturally into 2s and 4s; the whole crew won't fit at one counter."},{id:"daitokuji",name:"Daitoku-ji Zen Temple Complex",jp:"大徳寺",city:"Kyoto",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:3,desc:"A walled city of two dozen Zen sub-temples in northern Kyoto, several open to visitors: Daisen-in's celebrated dry-landscape garden 'paints' a whole river journey in gravel and stone, while Koto-in is a moss-and-maple corridor of pure calm. The birthplace of the formal tea ceremony, and almost tourist-free.",hours:"Sub-temples ~9:00–16:30 (each varies)",cost:"$3 (¥400) per sub-temple",station:"Bus to Daitokuji-mae / Kitaoji + walk",duration:"1.5–2h",decNote:"The serene, crowd-free Zen-garden counterweight to a Kinkaku-ji/Ryoan-ji morning on the same north-Kyoto bus loop. Gravel and moss read beautifully under flat winter light."},{id:"kyoto-tower",name:"Kyoto Tower",jp:"京都タワー",city:"Kyoto",category:"Viewpoint",tier:3,desc:"Kyoto's pointy 1964 landmark across from the station — a 131m observation deck giving a 360° view over a city that by law can't build tall, so the pagodas and the eastern mountains actually stand out. There's a cheap public bath in the basement (Kyoto Tower Daiyokujo) for the truly committed.",hours:"10:00–21:00 (last entry 20:30)",cost:"$5 (¥900) deck",station:"Kyoto Stn (across the street)",duration:"45 min",decNote:"December's clear air makes the mountain ring crisp; free binoculars pick out Kiyomizu and To-ji's pagoda lit up. A quick warm stop while waiting on a southbound shinkansen."},{id:"hozenji",name:"Hozenji Yokocho",jp:"法善寺横丁",city:"Osaka",category:"Nightlife",tier:2,desc:"Two tiny stone-paved lanes one block off the Dotonbori chaos, lit by paper lanterns and lined with old-Osaka bars and kappo counters. At the heart sits Mizukake Fudo — a moss-covered statue so drenched by visitors splashing it with prayer-water that it's now a solid green fuzzball. Splash it, then disappear into a counter.",hours:"Bars ~17:00–24:00; statue 24h",cost:"Free; eat/drink $19–38 (¥3,000–6,000)",station:"Namba (5 min walk)",duration:"1–2h",decNote:"The grown-up, lantern-lit antidote 90 seconds from Dotonbori's neon roar — duck in here when the crew wants atmosphere over spectacle on a cold night."},{id:"shinsaibashi",name:"Shinsaibashi-suji Arcade",jp:"心斎橋筋商店街",city:"Osaka",category:"Market & Shopping",tier:2,desc:"Osaka's 600m covered shopping spine running north from Dotonbori: drugstores, fashion, the giant Daimaru, Don Quijote, and a tax-free counter every fifty feet. Fully roofed, so it's the all-weather shopping artery that feeds straight into the Namba nightlife.",hours:"Shops ~11:00–21:00",cost:"Free to browse; wallet's problem",station:"Shinsaibashi (Midosuji line)",duration:"1–2h",decNote:"Covered and heated end to end — the obvious souvenir-and-drugstore run on a cold or wet Osaka afternoon, and it dumps you right at Dotonbori for dinner. Bring passports for tax-free."},{id:"tennoji-zoo",name:"Tennoji Park & Zoo",jp:"天王寺動物園",city:"Osaka",category:"Park & Nature",tier:3,desc:"A century-old zoo (one of Japan's oldest) and a green park wedged between Shinsekai and Abeno Harukas: lions, hippos, and a walk-through aviary, plus the open lawn 'Tennoji Park Entrance' (Ten-Shiba) lined with cafes. A genuinely central patch of outdoors in south Osaka.",hours:"Zoo 9:30–17:00, closed Mon",cost:"$3 (¥500) zoo; park free",station:"Tennoji / Dobutsuen-mae",duration:"1.5–2h",decNote:"Sits dead between Tsutenkaku, Shinsekai kushikatsu, and Abeno Harukas — an easy stitch into a south-Osaka afternoon. Winter animals are lively in the cold; the Ten-Shiba lawn does a small holiday illumination."},{id:"horyuji",name:"Horyu-ji Temple",jp:"法隆寺",city:"Nara",category:"Temple & Shrine",tier:3,desc:"The oldest wooden buildings on the planet — the five-story pagoda and main hall here have stood since around 700 AD, making this Japan's first UNESCO World Heritage site. The temple museum holds Asuka-period bronzes and the Kudara Kannon, an impossibly slender 7th-century masterpiece. The real cradle of Japanese Buddhism, an easy hop southwest of Nara.",hours:"8:00–16:30 (winter)",cost:"$10 (¥1,500) combined ticket",station:"Horyuji (JR Yamatoji line, ~12 min from Nara)",duration:"1.5–2h",decNote:"Far enough out that the December crowds evaporate entirely — you may have 1,300-year-old buildings nearly to yourself. A quiet, profound detour for the temple devotees; do it as a morning before the Nara Park deer circuit."},{id:"onomichi",name:"Onomichi — Temple Walk & Cat Alleys",jp:"尾道",city:"Hiroshima",category:"Landmark",tier:3,desc:"A photogenic hillside port on the Seto Inland Sea, an hour east of Hiroshima: a 25-temple walking trail up narrow lanes, a cat-filled 'Cat Alley,' a ropeway to a viewpoint over the strait, and the start of the Shimanami Kaido island-hopping cycle route. Onomichi ramen (soy-pork-back-fat) is the local religion.",hours:"Town always; ropeway ~9:00–17:15",cost:"Free to wander; ropeway $6 (¥700) round trip; ramen ~$6 (¥900)",station:"Onomichi (JR Sanyo line / shinkansen to Shin-Onomichi)",duration:"Half day",decNote:"Crisp, clear December views over the Inland Sea from Senkoji Park, near-empty lanes, and a hot bowl of Onomichi ramen against the cold. A mellow alternative or add-on to the heavier Hiroshima/Miyajima day for the crew that wants slow over solemn."},{id:"tomonoura",name:"Tomonoura Port Town",jp:"鞆の浦",city:"Hiroshima",category:"Landmark",tier:3,desc:"A perfectly preserved Edo-era fishing port on the Inland Sea, the only one in Japan that still has its original lantern lighthouse (joyato), stone wharf, and ship-chandler streets. Miyazaki lived here while drawing 'Ponyo,' and Wolverine fought ninja here in 'The Wolverine' — a tiny, time-frozen, ferry-served harbor.",hours:"Town always; shops/museums ~9:00–17:00",cost:"Free to wander; bus from Fukuyama ~$3 (¥530)",station:"Fukuyama (shinkansen) + 30-min bus to Tomonoura",duration:"Half day",decNote:"Quiet, windswept, and atmospheric in winter — warm up with homon-shu (the local medicinal sweet sake) the port has brewed for centuries. A deep-cut detour off the Fukuyama shinkansen stop for the crew chasing old-Japan over crowds."},{id:"amanohashidate",name:"Amanohashidate Sandbar",jp:"天橋立",city:"Day Trips",category:"Viewpoint",tier:3,desc:"One of Japan's three official 'great views' (with Miyajima and Matsushima): a 3.6km pine-covered sandbar arcing across a bay so that, viewed upside-down between your legs from the hilltop, it looks like a 'bridge to heaven.' Up north on the Sea of Japan coast, reached via the scenic Kyoto Tango Railway.",hours:"Viewpoint cablecar ~9:00–17:00",cost:"$6 (¥850) chairlift/cablecar; bend-over view free",station:"Amanohashidate (Kyoto Tango Railway, ~2h from Kyoto)",duration:"Full day from Kyoto",decNote:"A long winter haul north, but December gives clear Sea-of-Japan air and zero crowds on the sandbar. The mandatory 'matanozoki' upside-down look is undignified and non-negotiable. Pairs with Ine no Funaya up the coast if you have a full day."},{id:"ine-funaya",name:"Ine no Funaya Boat Houses",jp:"伊根の舟屋",city:"Day Trips",category:"Landmark",tier:3,desc:"A fishing village on the Sea of Japan where ~230 funaya 'boat houses' line the bay edge-to-edge — wooden homes built directly over the water with a boat garage on the ground floor and living quarters above. A sightseeing boat (or sea taxi) loops the bay so you see the whole row from the water, gulls dive-bombing for shrimp crackers.",hours:"Boat tours ~9:00–16:00; village always",cost:"Bay boat tour ~$6 (¥1,000); village free",station:"Bus from Amanohashidate (~1h) / far north of Kyoto",duration:"Full day from Kyoto",decNote:"Remote and very cold on the winter coast, but starkly beautiful and crowd-free — best stitched onto Amanohashidate for one big Sea-of-Japan day. Check the limited winter bus schedule before committing; service is sparse."},{id:"yoshinoyama",name:"Mt. Yoshino",jp:"吉野山",city:"Nara",category:"Park & Nature",tier:3,desc:"A sacred mountain ridge south of Nara, world-famous for 30,000 cherry trees in spring — but year-round it's a UNESCO pilgrimage town strung along the slope: the colossal Kinpusen-ji temple (Japan's second-largest wooden building), mountain-ascetic Shugendo history, and ryokan with valley views. A cablecar lifts you off the train into the village.",hours:"Kinpusen-ji ~8:30–16:00",cost:"Kinpusen-ji $5 (¥800); cablecar $3 (¥450)",station:"Yoshino (Kintetsu, ~1h15 from Nara/Osaka) + cablecar",duration:"Full day",decNote:"Bare and silent in December — no blossoms, but no crowds either, and a snow-dusted Kinpusen-ji is genuinely awesome. A deep-cut full-day pilgrimage for the temple obsessives; pack real winter layers, the ridge is cold and exposed."}],a=[{name:"Shinjuku",jp:"新宿",city:"Tokyo",vibe:"Neon maximalism — the Tokyo of your imagination",knownFor:["World's busiest station (3.6M/day)","Golden Gai & Omoide Yokocho","Kabukicho nightlife","Department store food basements"],proTip:"Learn ONE exit (the East). The station has 200+ — agreeing on 'meet at Shinjuku station' has ended friendships. In December the depachika halls go full New Year-gift mode; bento gets discounted after 19:00."},{name:"Shibuya",jp:"渋谷",city:"Tokyo",vibe:"Youth culture HQ, scramble crossing, big-screen energy",knownFor:["Scramble Crossing","Shibuya Sky","Center Gai nightlife","Parco's Nintendo/Pokémon floor"],proTip:"The backstreets (Dogenzaka, Udagawacho) have the actual good restaurants — the crossing block itself is mostly chains. Book Shibuya Sky's 15:30 slot to catch the ~16:30 December sunset and Fuji silhouette in one ticket ($17 / ¥2,700)."},{name:"Harajuku / Omotesando",jp:"原宿・表参道",city:"Tokyo",vibe:"Teen chaos on one street, high-fashion calm on the next",knownFor:["Takeshita Street","Cat Street vintage","Meiji Shrine next door","Omotesando champagne illumination"],proTip:"Do Meiji Shrine at 9am (it shuts at the ~16:30 dusk), Takeshita at 10 when shops open, escape down Cat Street before the noon crush. Come back after dark for Omotesando's gold winter lights."},{name:"Asakusa",jp:"浅草",city:"Tokyo",vibe:"Old Edo — temples, rickshaws, craftsman shops",knownFor:["Senso-ji","Nakamise snacks","Kappabashi kitchen street","Sumida river Skytree views"],proTip:"Stay past 18:00 when tour groups vanish — lit-up Senso-ji with empty grounds is a different temple. Late December the Hagoita-ichi (decorated battledore) market fills the temple grounds."},{name:"Ginza",jp:"銀座",city:"Tokyo",vibe:"Polished luxury, depachika food halls, gallery quiet",knownFor:["Uniqlo 12-floor flagship","Itoya stationery (18 floors)","Mitsukoshi food basement","Kabuki-za theater"],proTip:"Weekend afternoons the main street closes to cars ('Pedestrian Paradise'). Depachika basements discount bento after 19:00. December = the whole avenue lit up; the Ginza-dori illumination is free and classy."},{name:"Akihabara",jp:"秋葉原",city:"Tokyo",vibe:"Anime, arcades, electronics — otaku ground zero",knownFor:["Super Potato retro games","Multi-floor arcades","Gachapon halls","Mandarake & maid cafés"],proTip:"Arcades: floor 1 is crane games for tourists; rhythm games and retro cabinets live on floors 3+. Year-end sales start late December — buy the big-plug electronics here at the tax-free counter with your passport."},{name:"Ueno / Yanaka",jp:"上野・谷中",city:"Tokyo",vibe:"Museums, market bustle, then suddenly old-town quiet",knownFor:["Tokyo National Museum","Ameyoko street market","Yanaka Ginza","Nezu Shrine & temple cats"],proTip:"Ameyoko under the train tracks sells everything from tuna to sneakers — best chaos in late December as locals stock up for New Year. Yanaka Ginza's west-facing 'sunset stairs' frame the ~16:30 winter sun."},{name:"Roppongi / Azabudai",jp:"六本木・麻布台",city:"Tokyo",vibe:"Art towers, expat bars, and the city's best illuminations",knownFor:["Mori Art Museum (53F)","teamLab Borderless (Azabudai Hills)","Keyakizaka illumination","Late-night bars"],proTip:"The Keyakizaka blue-white LED tunnel with Tokyo Tower glowing behind it is THE December photo — lit at dusk through Christmas. Book teamLab Borderless 2–4 weeks out ($24–30 / ¥3,800–4,800) and stack it onto a Tokyo Tower evening."},{name:"Shimokitazawa",jp:"下北沢",city:"Tokyo",vibe:"Vintage shops, live houses, third-wave coffee — Tokyo's Brooklyn before Brooklyn",knownFor:["Vintage clothing maze","Tiny live-music venues","Curry shop density record","Reload & Mikan complexes"],proTip:"8 min from Shibuya on the Inokashira line, tap in with Suica. If a free afternoon appears, the thrift-store crawl here beats another hour anywhere crowded. Warm curry counters are the December move."},{name:"Nakano",jp:"中野",city:"Tokyo",vibe:"Broadway's otaku warren + a roaring local izakaya north exit",knownFor:["Nakano Broadway (30+ Mandarake stores)","Sun Mall arcade","Cheap izakaya alleys","5 min from Shinjuku"],proTip:"The izakaya lanes north of the station are Shinjuku prices minus 30% with zero tourists — strong audible for a low-key dinner night. Covered arcade keeps the December cold off you."},{name:"Ebisu / Daikanyama",jp:"恵比寿・代官山",city:"Tokyo",vibe:"Grown-up Tokyo: standing bars, gallery streets, the beautiful people",knownFor:["Ebisu Yokocho","Daikanyama T-Site bookstore","Craft beer bars","Yebisu Beer roots"],proTip:"One stop from Shibuya. Ebisu Yokocho at 19:00 then bar-hop west — the night out for when Kabukicho feels like too much television. Yebisu Garden Place runs a huge Baccarat chandelier illumination all December."},{name:"Kichijoji & Inokashira",jp:"吉祥寺",city:"Tokyo",vibe:"Park-side perfect neighborhood Tokyoites vote #1 to live in",knownFor:["Inokashira Park","Harmonica Yokocho alley","Ghibli Museum next door","Satou menchi-katsu queue"],proTip:"Pairs with the Ghibli Museum finale option — Harmonica Yokocho's micro-bars warm up and light up at the ~16:30 dusk, a hidden top-5 Tokyo drinking experience."},{name:"Toyosu / Odaiba",jp:"豊洲・お台場",city:"Tokyo",vibe:"Reclaimed bayfront — fish market, digital art, Gundam, Rainbow Bridge",knownFor:["teamLab Planets","Toyosu Market & tuna auction","Life-size moving Gundam","Rainbow Bridge views"],proTip:"teamLab Planets ($24–26 / ¥3,800–4,200) is here — book morning slots on the official DMM store 2–3 weeks out. The bayfront is wide open for clear Fuji-at-sunset shots across the water, but it's brutally windy in December."},{name:"Marunouchi (Tokyo Station)",jp:"丸の内",city:"Tokyo",vibe:"Red-brick grandeur, free rooftop views, and Tokyo's classiest illumination",knownFor:["1914 red-brick station","KITTE rooftop (free)","Naka-dori champagne-gold lights","Shinkansen departure point"],proTip:"Free, queue-free, and one of Tokyo's best December illuminations — the Naka-dori lights run from ~15:00. This is also where you board the SmartEX shinkansen south to Kyoto/Osaka, so wander it while you wait for the train."},{name:"Gion & Higashiyama",jp:"祇園・東山",city:"Kyoto",vibe:"Wooden teahouses, lantern light, geiko sightings",knownFor:["Hanamikoji Street","Shirakawa canal","Kiyomizu-dera slopes","Yasaka Shrine"],proTip:"The Sannenzaka/Ninenzaka lanes empty out after 17:00 — with the ~16:30 sunset, the sunset-to-dusk window here beats any daytime visit. Never block or chase geiko; the fines are real now."},{name:"Downtown Kyoto (Kawaramachi)",jp:"河原町",city:"Kyoto",vibe:"Where Kyoto actually eats and shops",knownFor:["Nishiki Market","Pontocho Alley","Teramachi arcades","Kamo riverbank"],proTip:"Pontocho looks expensive but has plenty of $19–25 (¥3,000–4,000) izakaya — look for picture menus on the north end. The covered Teramachi/Shinkyogoku arcades are your warm escape from the December cold."},{name:"Arashiyama",jp:"嵐山",city:"Kyoto",vibe:"River, bamboo, mountains — Kyoto's scenic west edge",knownFor:["Bamboo Grove","Togetsukyo Bridge","Monkey Park","%Arabica coffee"],proTip:"Everything closes early (~17:00). Go at dawn for an empty grove, be done by 14:00, head back for downtown evening. Mid-December the Hanatouro lantern event sometimes lights the grove and river after dark."},{name:"Kyoto Station South (Fushimi)",jp:"伏見",city:"Kyoto",vibe:"Torii gates and sake breweries",knownFor:["Fushimi Inari","Sake district tastings","Tofuku-ji"],proTip:"Fushimi's sake breweries (Gekkeikan Okura museum, ~$4 / ¥600 with tasting) are 10 min from the shrine and nobody goes. Hit Inari at the ~6:50 December sunrise to beat the buses, then warm up with sake."},{name:"Pontocho & Kiyamachi",jp:"先斗町・木屋町",city:"Kyoto",vibe:"Lantern alley elegance backed by a canal of cheap student bars",knownFor:["300m of wooden restaurants","Canal-side izakaya","Jankara karaoke","Kamogawa riverbank seating"],proTip:"Pontocho for the walk and one atmospheric drink; Kiyamachi for the actual night. The riverbank stairs at Sanjo bridge are the free afterparty — though December cold sends everyone indoors fast."},{name:"Nishiki & Teramachi",jp:"錦・寺町",city:"Kyoto",vibe:"Market artery + covered shopping arcades — Kyoto's daily engine",knownFor:["Nishiki Market (400m of food)","Teramachi arcade","Knife shops (Aritsugu)","Retro game stores upstairs"],proTip:"Nishiki stalls start closing 17:00–18:00 — it's a daytime mission, wonderfully chaotic with late-Dec osechi shoppers. Aritsugu knives get hand-engraved with your name while you wait (from ~$60 / ¥10,000)."},{name:"Kyoto Station Area",jp:"京都駅",city:"Kyoto",vibe:"Soaring glass terminal that's a destination in itself",knownFor:["Hara Hiroshi mega-station","Free rooftop sky garden","Ramen Koji floor","Nishi Hongan-ji nearby"],proTip:"The giant LED 'Graphical Lighting' staircase show runs nightly in December — free, and weirdly great. This is the SmartEX shinkansen hub; the Ramen Koji floor and free rooftop garden kill time before any train."},{name:"Namba / Dotonbori",jp:"難波・道頓堀",city:"Osaka",vibe:"Neon, noise, and the best street food in Japan",knownFor:["Glico sign","Hozenji Yokocho alley","Kuromon market","Den Den Town"],proTip:"Hozenji Yokocho — one alley south of the chaos — is a stone-paved pocket of tiny bars around a moss-covered Buddha. Magic. December crab season means the giant mechanical-crab restaurants are actually worth it now."},{name:"Umeda / Kita",jp:"梅田",city:"Osaka",vibe:"Skyscrapers, mega-malls, underground labyrinths",knownFor:["Umeda Sky Building","Grand Front dining","Whisky bars","HEP FIVE red ferris wheel"],proTip:"Umeda's underground city is genuinely maze-tier. Navigate by department store names, not compass directions. Time Umeda Sky ($13 / ¥2,000) for the ~16:50 sunset, then hit the German-style Christmas market at the building's base."},{name:"Shinsekai / Tennoji",jp:"新世界・天王寺",city:"Osaka",vibe:"Retro grit, kushikatsu, working-class soul",knownFor:["Tsutenkaku tower","Daruma kushikatsu","Spa World","Abeno Harukas (300m tower)"],proTip:"Janjan Yokocho alley has the cheapest, most local kushikatsu counters — half Daruma's price, same oil-crisped joy. One rule everywhere: no double-dipping the communal sauce. Abeno Harukas 300 ($13 / ¥2,000) is five minutes away for the sunset."},{name:"Nipponbashi / Den Den Town",jp:"日本橋",city:"Osaka",vibe:"Osaka's Akihabara, walking distance from Namba",knownFor:["Retro game shops","Figure & card stores","Kuromon Market on its edge","Otaku Road"],proTip:"Often cheaper than Akihabara for identical retro carts — buy here, skip the Tokyo markup. It's a 10-minute walk from Amerikamura, so split the group and reconvene at the Glico sign."},{name:"Amerikamura",jp:"アメリカ村",city:"Osaka",vibe:"Streetwear, vintage, clubs — Kansai youth culture's living room",knownFor:["Triangle Park people-watching","Vintage/streetwear shops","Game bars","Big Step complex"],proTip:"Hypebeast squad goes here while the nerds hit Den Den Town — it's a 10-minute walk between them, reconvene at the giant Glico sign. Racks are cheaper than Tokyo's."},{name:"Osaka Bay (Minato)",jp:"大阪港",city:"Osaka",vibe:"Harbor district — aquarium, giant Ferris wheel, USJ across the water",knownFor:["Kaiyukan aquarium","Tempozan Ferris wheel","Legoland Discovery Center","Ferry to USJ"],proTip:"Solid wet-or-freezing-day plan: Kaiyukan ($17 / ¥2,700) then the Tempozan wheel, both lit up after the ~16:50 December sunset. The Captain Line ferry shortcuts across the bay to Universal City."},{name:"Nara Park",jp:"奈良公園",city:"Nara",vibe:"Sacred deer, giant Buddha, and lantern-lined forest shrines",knownFor:["1,200 bowing sika deer","Todai-ji Great Buddha","Kasuga Taisha lanterns","Kofuku-ji pagoda"],proTip:"Crackers are ~$1 (¥200); the deer are polite until they smell paper, so zip your tickets and maps away. Winter coats make them extra round, and thin December crowds mean more deer attention per person."},{name:"Naramachi",jp:"ならまち",city:"Nara",vibe:"Preserved merchant lanes of lattice-front machiya houses",knownFor:["Old townhouse cafés","Harushika sake brewery","Craft & pickle shops","Quiet between temples"],proTip:"The 5-sake tasting at Harushika brewery (~$3 / ¥500) is a justified warm-up detour after the cold temple circuit. Quietest, most local stretch of Nara — a calm half-hour off the deer-park bustle."},{name:"Kamakura",jp:"鎌倉",city:"Day Trips",vibe:"Seaside temple town — the Dec 19 day trip anchor",knownFor:["Great Buddha (Kotoku-in)","Hase-dera ocean terrace","Tsurugaoka Hachimangu","Komachi-dori snack street"],proTip:"An hour from Tokyo, all on Suica — no JR Pass needed. Do the Great Buddha and Hase-dera together (7 min apart on the Enoden), then ride the coastal tram to Enoshima for sunset. Komachi-dori is the warm-snack engine of the day."},{name:"Enoshima",jp:"江の島",city:"Day Trips",vibe:"Causeway island of shrine caves and the best Fuji-over-the-sea sunset near Tokyo",knownFor:["Sea Candle lighthouse-garden","Enoshima Shrine & caves","Fuji over Sagami Bay","Whitebait (shirasu) bowls"],proTip:"December gives the clearest Fuji-over-the-ocean sunset of the year. The winter Sea Candle illumination ('Shonan no Hoseki') is shockingly good — grab the ~$7 (¥1,100) island/escalator/Sea Candle pass and stay for dark. Natural finale of the Dec 19 trip."},{name:"Miyajima",jp:"宮島",city:"Day Trips",vibe:"Sacred island: floating torii, mountain shrines, opportunist deer",knownFor:["Itsukushima floating torii","Grilled oysters (peak December)","Momiji manju","Mt. Misen ropeway"],proTip:"Check the tide chart the night before: plan to see the torii at BOTH high tide (floating) and low tide (walk to its feet) in one visit window. December = oyster season, so come hungry — the grilled ones at the stalls are the move."},{name:"Uji",jp:"宇治",city:"Day Trips",vibe:"Matcha capital + the temple on the ¥10 coin + Nintendo's museum",knownFor:["Byodo-in temple","Stone-milled matcha everything","Nintendo Museum","Tale of Genji ending"],proTip:"Tsuen (1160 AD) by the bridge is the world's oldest tea house — hot matcha there, warm parfaits anywhere, exactly what a near-freezing day wants. The Nintendo Museum ($22 / ¥3,500) next door is a monthly lottery; every one of the 8 should enter the same date."},{name:"Himeji",jp:"姫路",city:"Day Trips",vibe:"One reason to come: the greatest original castle in Japan",knownFor:["Himeji Castle (the White Heron)","Koko-en garden","15-min walk straight off the station","SmartEX shinkansen hop"],proTip:"Brilliant-white walls against a clear blue December sky — bring sunglasses, seriously. Book the shinkansen leg on SmartEX; it's a quick hop past Kobe, easy to pair with a Kobe-beef lunch and an Arima onsen soak."},{name:"Kobe",jp:"神戸",city:"Day Trips",vibe:"Harbor city of certified beef, European mansions, and December lights",knownFor:["Certified Kobe beef teppanyaki","Harborland & Mosaic","Kitano foreign houses","Kobe Luminarie"],proTip:"20 minutes from Osaka. Teppanyaki lunch sets run $38–94 (¥6,000–15,000) — half the dinner price for the same beef. The Kobe Luminarie memorial illumination lights up in December; check exact 2026 dates and time the harbor walk for after the ~16:50 sunset."}],t=[{id:"kamakura-enoshima",name:"Kamakura + Enoshima",jp:"鎌倉・江の島",base:"Tokyo",travel:"Point-to-point on Suica/PASMO, NO rail pass: JR Yokosuka / Shonan-Shinjuku line Shinjuku or Tokyo→Kamakura direct, ~60 min, ¥960 ($6); inside the area the 1902 Enoden tram hops temple-to-temple (tap your IC card, or the 'Noriorikun' day pass ¥700/$4.40 pays off after ~3 rides); return from Katase-Enoshima on the Odakyu line to Shinjuku ~70 min ¥660 ($4.10), or Enoden back to Kamakura + JR. Forward nothing — this is a day bag, IC-card, no-reservation kind of trip.",cost:"~$50/person (trains ~$15 + Great Buddha + Hase-dera + Enoshima island pass + shirasu lunch + street food)",hours:"08:00–19:30",tier:1,pitch:"THE Dec 19 plan: the samurai capital by the sea, one hour south. A 13-meter open-air bronze Buddha you can stand INSIDE, a hillside temple staring down the Pacific, and a rattling 110-year-old green tram threading between surf shops and backyards. Cap it on Enoshima island, where a crisp, dry December evening hands you the single best Fuji-over-the-ocean sunset anywhere near Tokyo — volcano silhouette across Sagami Bay, then the Sea Candle lighthouse lit up for winter. Replaces the old Hakone day entirely, costs half as much, and never once asks you to look at a rail pass.",play:["08:00 — JR Yokosuka/Shonan-Shinjuku line from Shinjuku or Tokyo (grab egg sandos + a hot coffee for the ride; tap in with Suica, no reservation, no pass)","09:05 — Kamakura station: walk Komachi-dori as the snack shops roll up their shutters (sweet-potato soft serve and warm ningyo-yaki at 9am is fully legal here)","09:40 — Tsurugaoka Hachimangu: the Minamoto shogunate's great shrine crowning the avenue, wide stone approach (dankazura) and a giant ginkgo, near-empty at this hour","10:30 — Enoden tram from Kamakura to Hase (4 stops — the tiny green tram squeezing between living-room windows is half the reason you came)","10:50 — The Great Buddha at Kotoku-in (¥300/$1.90, +¥50 to climb INSIDE the 1252 bronze casting and see the hollow shell) — open-air since a 1498 tsunami took the hall","11:50 — Hase-dera (¥400/$2.50): the 9m gilded eleven-headed Kannon, thousands of tiny red-bibbed jizo, and a hillside terrace looking straight down Yuigahama beach to the bay","12:50 — Shirasu-don lunch near Hase (raw or boiled whitebait over rice — the Shonan coast's signature; ~¥1,300/$8)","14:00 — Enoden the rest of the way down the coast to Enoshima — December-clear days throw Fuji up over the water out the right-hand windows, between the houses, for about 40 seconds at Kamakurakohan","14:40 — Cross the causeway bridge to Enoshima: climb past Enoshima Shrine, ride the outdoor escalators ('Escar') up, hit the Iwaya sea caves at the back of the island (¥500/$3.10, candle-lit lava tubes the waves carved)","16:00 — Be at the Sea Candle observation tower (¥800/$5, or the ¥1,100/$6.90 island combo covers caves + garden + tower + escalators) for the December alpenglow","16:35 — Fuji silhouette goes black against an orange Sagami Bay at sunset; the Samuel Cocking Garden + Sea Candle winter illumination switches on after dark, then Odakyu home from Katase-Enoshima"],protip:"Sunset is ~16:35 in mid-December and the light show is FAST — build the whole day backwards from standing on Enoshima's west side by 16:10. Mornings near the coast hover just above freezing and the wind off the bay bites, so this is a real-coat day even though it's at sea level. Black kites (tonbi) divebomb street food along this entire coast — eat with your back to a wall, hold the senbei low. Weekday beats weekend by a mile; the Enoden gets sardine-packed on December weekends chasing the same Fuji sunset you are.",links:[{label:"Kotoku-in (Great Buddha) official",url:"https://www.kotoku-in.jp/en/"},{label:"Hase-dera official",url:"https://www.hasedera.jp/en/"},{label:"Enoden railway (English)",url:"https://www.enoden.co.jp/en/"},{label:"Enoshima Sea Candle / Cocking Garden",url:"https://enoshima-seacandle.com/"}],wiki:"Kōtoku-in"},{id:"nikko",name:"Nikko — Toshogu in Winter",jp:"日光",base:"Tokyo",travel:"Point-to-point, NO rail pass: Tobu limited express (Revaty Kegon / Spacia X) Asakusa→Tobu-Nikko, ~1h50, ~¥3,050 ($19) each way with reserved seat — buy seats on the Tobu Top Tours / Tobu Railway site in advance; or pay-as-you-go on local Tobu trains + area buses with your IC card and just add the limited express seat fee each way to skip the slow ride. (Tobu, not JR — your Suica/PASMO taps the buses too.)",cost:"~$70/person (trains ~$38 + Toshogu ¥1,600/$10 + yuba lunch + buses)",hours:"07:00–19:00",tier:3,pitch:"Tokugawa Ieyasu's mausoleum is the most extravagant thing ever built in Japan — a mountain forest of 350-year-old cedars wrapped around shrines dripping in gold leaf and over 5,000 carvings. In late December there's a real chance the whole complex is dusted in snow, which turns the gold up to eleven and the crowds down to nothing, and the cold thins the day-tripper herd to almost zero. The longest haul in the Tokyo deck, and it earns every minute. UNESCO World Heritage, and it shows.",play:["07:00 — Revaty/Spacia X limited express from Asakusa (reserve seats in advance; sleep, or stare out at a frozen Kanto plain — buy a hot coffee at the kiosk)","09:00 — Tobu-Nikko station: bus up the hill (tap your IC card) past the Shinkyo sacred bridge","09:30 — Toshogu (¥1,600/$10): the Yomeimon 'sunset gate' (so detailed people once stared at it till dusk — hence the name), the original see-no-evil-hear-no-evil-speak-no-evil monkeys, the famous sleeping cat (nemuri-neko), then 207 stone steps up to Ieyasu's surprisingly quiet bronze tomb","11:30 — Futarasan Shrine and Taiyuin: the grandson Iemitsu's deliberately humbler mausoleum tucked in the cedars — many people's secret favorite, and far quieter than Toshogu","12:45 — Yuba lunch (yuba = tofu skin, in every conceivable form — Nikko's mountain-temple monk specialty, weirdly excellent, and hot food is the move at near-freezing altitude)","14:00 — Kanmangafuchi Abyss: a lava-gorge riverside lined with ~70 jizo statues in red knit caps (the 'Bake-jizo' — count them twice and you'll get a different number, allegedly), dead silent and dusted with snow in winter","15:00 — Rinno-ji temple's Sanbutsudo hall and the gold Buddhas, if legs allow","15:45 — Shinkyo bridge photo (vermillion arch over an icy river), sweet shops, and warm yuba manju or castella cake for the train","16:30 — Limited express home; Asakusa by ~18:30"],protip:"It runs a brutal 5–8°C colder than Tokyo and the shaded shrine paths hold ice all day — this is the trip for every single layer you packed, plus grip soles. Skip the Lake Chuzenji / Kegon Falls extension in December (the Irohazaka switchback road ices over, buses get cut, and it eats 2+ hours); the World Heritage cluster in town IS the show. Buy limited express seats both ways in advance — winter trains run short and the good ones sell out. The Tobu Nikko Pass covers the round trip + local buses on your card, no JR involved.",links:[{label:"Nikko Toshogu official",url:"https://www.toshogu.jp/"},{label:"Nikko Toshogu (English)",url:"https://www.toshogu.jp/english/"},{label:"Tobu Railway passes (English)",url:"https://www.tobu.co.jp/en/"}],wiki:"Nikkō Tōshō-gū"},{id:"kawaguchiko",name:"Kawaguchiko — Closest Fuji Day",jp:"河口湖",base:"Tokyo",travel:"Highway bus from Busta Shinjuku (the terminal above Shinjuku station, gate 4F)→Kawaguchiko Station, ~1h45, ~¥2,200 ($14) each way — reserve seats online up to a month out (no rail pass exists for this anyway); first departures ~6:45 sell the best Fuji light. The slower alternative is JR/Fujikyu trains paid by IC card, but the bus is faster and cheaper.",cost:"~$60/person (bus ~$28 + local trains/ropeway ~$18 + hoto lunch)",hours:"06:45–19:00",tier:3,pitch:"The Fuji Five Lakes put you at the mountain's feet: Chureito Pagoda's five-story-pagoda-plus-Fuji postcard, the lake panorama from the ropeway, and the volcano filling your entire windshield from 15km out. December is statistically the best month of the year to actually SEE Fuji — cold, dry, ruthless clarity, and the snowcap is finally on. This is the all-in photo day, and it only runs on a clear forecast — check the webcam the night before or don't go.",play:["06:45 — Highway bus from Busta Shinjuku (left/passenger side seats for the Fuji reveal as the expressway crests; sunrise Fuji over the wing of the bus is the warmup)","08:45 — Kawaguchiko Station: Fuji status check, lockers for anything heavy, hot vending-machine coffee","09:15 — Fujikyu line two stops to Shimoyoshida → climb the 398 steps to Chureito Pagoda (THE postcard: vermillion five-story pagoda, town below, snow-capped Fuji filling the sky behind — and in December, no autumn-leaf tour-bus mob)","11:15 — Back to Kawaguchiko; hoto lunch (flat hand-cut udon in a pumpkin-miso stew served boiling in an iron pot — Yamanashi's winter weapon, ~¥1,200/$8)","12:45 — Mt. Fuji Panoramic Ropeway (the 'Kachi Kachi Yama' ropeway) up Mt. Tenjo (~¥1,800/$11 round trip) for the lake-and-Fuji panorama and the tanuki-and-rabbit folktale photo spots","14:15 — Bus or rental cycle to Oishi Park on the north shore — THE calendar-photo angle, Fuji mirrored across Lake Kawaguchi with winter flowers in the foreground","15:45 — Lakeside coffee and a Fujiyama Cookie back near the station to thaw out","16:30 — Watch the December alpenglow ('beni-Fuji') turn the snowcap pink (~16:30), then the bus home — Shinjuku by ~18:30"],protip:"Book a CANCELLABLE bus and decide the night before off the forecast and live webcams — Fuji hidden = day wasted, Fuji out = the best photos of the entire trip. It's a high-altitude basin (~830m): noticeably colder than Tokyo, dawn well below freezing, and the pagoda steps are sheet ice in the morning shade — grip soles or you're going down them on your back. Buses back to Shinjuku sell out Sunday and holiday evenings; reserve the return seat too. If two clear days line up, this and Kamakura+Enoshima are your two Fuji angles — ocean vs lake.",links:[{label:"Highway Bus reservations (Keio)",url:"https://www.highwaybus.com/gp/index"},{label:"Mt. Fuji Panoramic Ropeway",url:"https://www.mtfujiropeway.jp/en/"},{label:"Fujikyu Railway (English)",url:"https://www.fujikyu-railway.jp/en/"}],wiki:"Lake Kawaguchi"},{id:"yokohama",name:"Yokohama — Ramen, Chinatown & Harbor Lights",jp:"横浜",base:"Tokyo",travel:"Point-to-point on Suica/PASMO, no pass: Tokyu Shin-Yokohama line Shibuya→Shin-Yokohama direct, ~25 min, ~¥470 ($2.90) to start at the Ramen Museum; then local trains / Minatomirai line between districts (¥200–300/$1.30–1.90 a hop, just tap); Tokyu Toyoko line back to Shibuya from Motomachi-Chukagai, ~40 min, ¥550 ($3.40).",cost:"~$45/person (trains ~$9 + museums ~$9 + ramen grazing + Christmas market beers)",hours:"10:30–21:00",tier:3,pitch:"Japan's second city is 30 minutes away and built for a low-effort, high-calorie day: a retro-streetscape museum where you crawl nine regional ramen styles in mini-bowls, the country's biggest Chinatown, and a harbor skyline that does December better than almost anywhere — Christmas market, skating rink, full illumination. The best bad-weather or tired-legs card in the deck.",play:["10:30 — Tokyu direct from Shibuya to Shin-Yokohama","11:00 — Shin-Yokohama Ramen Museum (~¥450/$2.80 entry): a 1958 Tokyo streetscape underground with nine legendary regional shops — order mini-bowls (~¥600/$3.80 each) and hit three minimum","13:00 — Train to Motomachi-Chukagai: Yokohama Chinatown, 600+ shops — steamed buns and peking-duck wraps as dessert-after-ramen","14:30 — Yamashita Park stroll along the water past the Hikawa Maru liner (wind off the bay is cold — keep moving)","15:00 — Cup Noodles Museum (¥500/$3.10): design and seal your own custom Cup Noodle (~¥500/$3.10 extra) — the dumbest great souvenir in Japan","16:30 — Red Brick Warehouse: German-style Christmas Market in December (mulled wine, sausages, an outdoor skating rink) just as the lights come on at dusk","18:00 — Minato Mirai illumination walk + Landmark Tower Sky Garden observatory (¥1,000/$6.30, 273m) for the harbor-and-Fuji dusk view — clear December evenings put Fuji on the western horizon","19:30 — Dinner-and-a-beer in Noge, the old-school izakaya quarter, then Toyoko line home"],protip:"The Ramen Museum shops post their wait times at the door — split into pairs and divide-and-conquer different bowls, then compare notes. The Christmas market at the Red Brick Warehouse charges a small entry on December weekends and queues after 17:00; weekday evening is the cheat code.",links:[{label:"Shin-Yokohama Ramen Museum",url:"https://www.raumen.co.jp/english/"},{label:"Cup Noodles Museum Yokohama",url:"https://www.cupnoodles-museum.jp/en/yokohama/"},{label:"Yokohama Red Brick Warehouse",url:"https://www.yokohama-akarenga.jp/en/"}],wiki:"Minato Mirai 21"},{id:"kawagoe",name:"Kawagoe — Little Edo",jp:"川越",base:"Tokyo",travel:"Closest day trip in the deck, all on IC card: Tobu Tojo line express Ikebukuro→Kawagoe, ~30 min, ~¥480 ($3) each way; or the Seibu Shinjuku line to Hon-Kawagoe. The Kurazukuri warehouse street is a 10–15 min walk or a short ¥190/$1.20 Tobu bus hop from the station — tap your Suica on the bus.",cost:"~$30/person (trains ~$6 + Toki-no-Kane area is free + sweet-potato everything + craft beer + lunch)",hours:"09:30–18:00",tier:3,pitch:"Half an hour north of Ikebukuro sits a town that kept its Edo merchant streetscape: black clay-walled kurazukuri warehouses, a 400-year-old wooden bell tower (Toki-no-Kane) that still chimes, and an entire alley of penny-candy shops. It's the low-effort, low-cost, high-charm card — no mountains, no bullet train, no reservations, just a half-day wander with a beer and a sweet-potato soft serve. Sweet potato is the local obsession; lean into it.",play:["09:30 — Tobu Tojo express from Ikebukuro (grab a coffee, it's a short hop)","10:15 — Walk into the Kurazukuri (warehouse) district: the surviving Edo merchant street, clay-walled storehouses now full of shops","10:45 — Toki-no-Kane: the wooden bell tower over the rooftops, ~400 years old, chimes at noon — time the photo to the 12:00 ring","11:15 — Kashiya Yokocho ('Penny Candy Alley'): a lane of old-school sweet shops — fat sweet-potato chips, candy, and the longest sweet-potato soft serve you'll ever hold","12:30 — Lunch: unagi (Kawagoe's traditional specialty, ~¥2,500/$16) or sweet-potato-themed everything; Coedo craft beer (the local brewery, sweet-potato 'Beniaka' lager included) at a tap room","14:00 — Kawagoe Hikawa Shrine: the tunnel of ~1,500 pink and red wind chimes and the heart-shaped 'aitai' draw — quiet and pretty in winter light","15:00 — Kita-in temple: the only surviving rooms of Edo Castle (relocated here after a fire) and the 540 stone Gohyaku-Rakan statues, every face different","16:00 — Last warehouse-street wander as the December dusk and shop lanterns come on (~16:30 sunset), then the train back to Ikebukuro by ~17:30"],protip:"It's a flat, walkable half-day with zero altitude and no Fuji gamble — the perfect bad-forecast or recovery-day card, and the cheapest day in the whole deck. Mondays many of the old shops close, so aim Tue–Sun. Pair it with an Ikebukuro evening (Sunshine City, Pokémon Center MEGA) on the way back since you're returning to that exact station.",links:[{label:"Tobu Railway — Kawagoe guide",url:"https://www.tobu.co.jp/en/sightseeing/articles/27.html"},{label:"Koedo Kawagoe (official tourism)",url:"https://www.koedo.or.jp/foreign/english/"}],wiki:"Kawagoe"},{id:"nara",name:"Nara — Deer, the Great Buddha & Mochi Violence",jp:"奈良",base:"Kansai",travel:"Point-to-point on Suica/ICOCA, no pass: Kintetsu limited express Kyoto→Kintetsu-Nara, 35 min, ¥1,280 ($8) each way (¥760 fare + ¥520 reserved seat) — drops you a 5-min walk from the park; the ordinary express on the same line is ¥760 ($4.80) and only 10 min slower. Skip JR Nara; it's a 20-min walk further out.",cost:"~$35/person (trains ~$16 + Todai-ji ¥800/$5 + deer crackers, mochi, sake tasting)",hours:"12:30–19:30",tier:1,pitch:"Locked for the Dec 23 afternoon: 1,200 sacred deer that bow for crackers, a 500-ton bronze Buddha from 752 AD inside one of the largest wooden buildings on Earth, and the viral mochi-pounding shop — all within one walkable park. December means fluffy winter deer, thin crowds, and more bowing per cracker. An afternoon is genuinely enough.",play:["12:30 — Kintetsu limited express from Kyoto (buy reserved seats at the machine — all 8 together, big windows)","13:10 — Kintetsu-Nara station: straight up the Higashimuki arcade","13:20 — Nakatanidou: catch the high-speed mochi pounding if the hammers are flying (the two-man pounding show is a viral institution — they go absurdly fast), eat one warm yomogi mochi regardless (¥200/$1.30 — best ¥200 in Kansai)","13:45 — Into Nara Park: buy shika senbei (¥200/$1.30 a stack), get politely mobbed, hold a cracker high and collect a bow before you hand it over","14:30 — Todai-ji Daibutsuden (¥800/$5): the 15m, 500-ton bronze Great Buddha from 752 AD inside one of the largest wooden buildings on Earth — and the pillar with a hole the exact size of his nostril (crawl through = enlightenment, limber members only)","15:30 — Walk the stone-lantern path through the forest to Kasuga Taisha (grounds free; ~3,000 bronze and stone lanterns, deer included)","16:15 — Catch the December sunset (~16:45 in Nara) over the park from Nigatsu-do's terrace — the whole Nara basin lights up below the hall, near-empty in winter","16:45 — Naramachi old quarter: machiya lanes + Harushika brewery sake tasting (5 pours, ¥500/$3.10, keep the cup)","17:45 — Snacks/dinner near Higashimuki, then the limited express back — Kyoto by ~19:30"],protip:"The deer bow first and headbutt second — hide every piece of paper (maps and paper tickets smell like food to them, apparently, and they WILL eat your ticket). Hold senbei behind your back between feedings or you become the buffet. Winter deer are fluffier, calmer, and the crowds thin right out. Todai-ji's last entry is 16:30 in winter, so do the Buddha before the shrine, not after.",links:[{label:"Kintetsu Railway (English)",url:"https://www.kintetsu.co.jp/foreign/english/"},{label:"Todai-ji official",url:"https://www.todaiji.or.jp/"},{label:"Nara Park / Visit Nara",url:"https://www.visitnara.jp/"}],wiki:"Nara Park"},{id:"uji-nintendo",name:"Uji + Nintendo Museum",jp:"宇治・ニンテンドーミュージアム",base:"Kansai",travel:"All on IC card, no pass: JR Nara line rapid Kyoto→Uji, 17 min, ¥240 ($1.50). The Nintendo Museum sits beside Kintetsu Ogura station, a ~20-min walk (or short ¥800/$5 cab) west of the Byodo-in area — then ride Kintetsu south from Ogura via Yamato-Saidaiji straight into Osaka-Namba (~60 min, ~¥900/$5.60), luggage already forwarded by Yamato takkyubin.",cost:"~$45/person (museum ¥3,300/$21 + Byodo-in ¥700/$4.40 + trains ~$7 + matcha + Hatena Burger)",hours:"08:45–16:30",tier:1,pitch:"Locked for Dec 24 morning, engineered as the Kyoto→Osaka moving day: Japan's matcha capital plus the temple on the ¥10 coin in the morning, then the Nintendo Museum — every console the company ever made, back to the 1889 hanafuda cards, with giant-controller games you burn digital coins on. It's lottery-entry only, so the whole squad applies three months out and we pool the wins.",play:["08:45 — Send luggage Kyoto→Osaka by Yamato takkyubin (or coin-locker it at Kyoto station), then JR rapid to Uji","09:15 — Byodo-in (¥700/$4.40): the Phoenix Hall floating on its pond — the exact building on the back of the ¥10 coin, in clean, crisp December morning light","10:15 — Matcha on the temple approach: Nakamura Tokichi for the matcha-jelly parfait, or Tsuen by the bridge — pouring tea since 1160, often called the oldest tea house on Earth","11:00 — Cross the Uji river for a quick Ujigami Shrine hit (free; the oldest original shrine building in Japan, UNESCO-listed) if the timing's loose","11:30 — Walk or cab ~20 min west to the Nintendo Museum by Kintetsu Ogura","12:00 — Museum entry slot (¥3,300/$21; LOTTERY at museum-tickets.nintendo.com ~3 months ahead — all 8 apply for the same date/slot separately): upstairs is the full hardware timeline back to the 1889 hanafuda cards, no photos in half of it, pure nostalgia damage","13:00 — Burn your 10 digital coins downstairs: two-person giant NES controllers, Ultra Machine batting, zapper duels, hanafuda lessons — winner of Giant Mario claims bragging rights all the way through Osaka","14:30 — Late lunch at the museum's Hatena Burger (build-your-own; ~¥1,500/$9, reserve when you book entry)","15:15 — Kintetsu from Ogura→change Yamato-Saidaiji→Osaka-Namba: walk into the Namba apartment by ~16:30, Christmas Eve in Dotonbori ahead"],protip:"The lottery is the whole ballgame: applications open ~3 months before the visit month — set a calendar alarm for late September 2026, everyone applies, duplicate wins get released. If the lottery shuts us out entirely, the Uji half still works as a 3-hour matcha-and-temple stop and we're in Osaka by 13:00.",links:[{label:"Nintendo Museum tickets (lottery)",url:"https://museum-tickets.nintendo.com/en/"},{label:"Byodo-in official",url:"https://www.byodoin.or.jp/en/"},{label:"Nakamura Tokichi (matcha)",url:"https://global.tokichi.jp/"}],wiki:"Byōdō-in"},{id:"hiroshima-miyajima",name:"Hiroshima + Miyajima",jp:"広島・宮島",base:"Kansai",travel:"Nozomi shinkansen Shin-Osaka→Hiroshima, ~85 min, ~¥10,620 ($66) reserved each way — book all 8 seats together on the SmartEX app/site well ahead (no JR Pass; Dec 26 sits in peak New Year travel season, so reserved cars fill). In town: trams ¥220/$1.40 flat, tap your ICOCA; JR Sanyo line Hiroshima→Miyajimaguchi 25 min + 10-min JR ferry (~¥300/$1.90 incl. the ¥100 island visitor tax).",cost:"~$175/person (shinkansen ~$132 + museum/shrine/ferry ~$7 + okonomiyaki + oysters)",hours:"07:00–21:15",tier:1,pitch:"Locked Dec 26 and the heaviest, best day of the trip: the Peace Memorial Museum in the morning — devastating, essential, non-negotiable — then the spirit-lifting second act on Miyajima, where the vermillion torii rises out of the Seto Inland Sea and December means oyster season at its absolute peak. Two of Japan's most important places, one bullet train, home by nine.",play:["07:00 — Midosuji line to Shin-Osaka; Nozomi rolling by ~07:40 (konbini breakfast on the platform)","09:10 — Tram or 15-min walk to the Peace Memorial Park: approach the A-Bomb Dome first, from across the river, in quiet morning light","09:45 — Peace Memorial Museum (¥200/$1.30): give it two unhurried hours — artifacts, testimony, no flinching; this is why we came","12:00 — Cenotaph, eternal flame, and the Children's Peace Monument draped in a million folded paper cranes","12:45 — Okonomimura: three floors of griddle counters — Hiroshima-style okonomiyaki layered with yakisoba noodles (~¥1,200/$8), grab any open seat at a teppan","14:00 — JR Sanyo line to Miyajimaguchi (25 min), straight onto the JR ferry (10 min — stand on the right, starboard side, for the torii approach)","14:45 — Itsukushima Shrine (¥500/$3.10): the shrine on stilts and THE vermillion torii — timed to the tide if the gods cooperate (floating at high tide, walk-up-and-touch-it at low)","15:45 — Omotesando arcade: grilled oysters at the street stalls (December = peak season, fat and cheap at ¥500–800/$3–5 a pop) + momiji-manju fried fresh and hot","17:00 — Dusk torii against the lit water, then the 17:30–18:00 ferry back","19:15 — Nozomi home; Namba by ~21:15, legs done, perspective recalibrated"],protip:"Check the Miyajima tide table (on the official island site) the week before and flip the day if needed — museum-first vs island-first — to catch the torii BOTH floating and walkable. Book the Peace Museum timed entry online to skip the line, and don't schedule anything emotionally demanding for that evening; the museum stays with you.",links:[{label:"Hiroshima Peace Memorial Museum",url:"https://hpmmuseum.jp/"},{label:"Miyajima Tourist Association",url:"https://www.miyajima.or.jp/"},{label:"smartEX shinkansen booking",url:"https://smart-ex.jp/en/"}],wiki:"Itsukushima Shrine"},{id:"himeji-kobe",name:"Himeji + Kobe Combo",jp:"姫路・神戸",base:"Kansai",travel:"No pass needed: Nozomi/Sakura shinkansen Shin-Osaka→Himeji, ~30 min, ~¥3,280 ($21) unreserved each way (SmartEX or just buy at the machine); return in stages on your ICOCA — JR Special Rapid Himeji→Sannomiya (Kobe) 40 min ¥990/$6.20, then Sannomiya→Osaka 21 min ¥420/$2.60. Three cities, one JR line home.",cost:"~$130/person (trains ~$49 + castle combo ¥1,050/$6.60 + Kobe beef teppanyaki lunch ~¥8,000/$50)",hours:"08:15–19:00",tier:2,pitch:"The Dec 28 audible, and it's a banger: Japan's greatest surviving original castle — the White Heron, never burned, never bombed, never concrete — 30 minutes away by bullet train, then certified Kobe beef eaten in Kobe at half the dinner price, plus the hillside Kitano district on the walk-off. Castle, A5 wagyu, harbor dusk, back in Namba for the last big night.",play:["08:15 — Midosuji to Shin-Osaka; ~08:45 shinkansen west (unreserved cars are fine for a 30-min hop)","09:20 — Himeji station: the castle fills the avenue dead ahead — 15-min walk straight at it","09:45 — Himeji Castle (¥1,000/$6.30): six storeys of original 1609 keep, shoes-off ladder-stairs, arrow slits and stone-drop chutes — at opening you'll have it nearly to yourselves","11:30 — Koko-en gardens next door (combo ticket ¥1,050/$6.60 covers both): nine Edo-style walled gardens against the white castle backdrop","12:20 — JR Special Rapid east to Sannomiya (40 min — same Inland Sea view the shinkansen charges triple for)","13:15 — Kobe beef teppanyaki LUNCH in Sannomiya: lunch sets run ¥6,000–10,000/$38–63 vs ¥20,000+/$125+ at dinner — book ahead for 8, or split across two counters and trade bites","15:00 — Kitano-Ijinkan: Meiji-era foreign merchants' mansions on the hillside, Starbucks-in-a-heritage-house included","16:15 — Nankinmachi Chinatown snack lap, then Kobe Harborland as the port tower and Ferris wheel light up (~17:00 December dusk)","18:15 — JR back to Osaka — showered and in Ura-Namba by 20:00 for the farewell crawl"],protip:"Himeji Castle's last keep entry is 16:00 and it closes Dec 29–30 for New Year — the 28th is literally the last possible day, which is why this audible exists. White walls against December blue sky are blinding: sunglasses are gear, not vibes. Kobe Luminarie has shifted to late January in recent years, so don't bank on it — Harborland's regular lights carry the evening fine.",links:[{label:"Himeji Castle official",url:"https://www.himejicastle.jp/"},{label:"Feel Kobe (official tourism)",url:"https://www.feel-kobe.jp/"}],wiki:"Himeji Castle"},{id:"minoo-falls",name:"Minoo Falls",jp:"箕面大滝",base:"Kansai",travel:"Hankyu Takarazuka line Osaka-Umeda→Ishibashi handai-mae (change cross-platform to the Minoo line)→Minoh, ~30 min total, ¥280 ($1.80) each way, tap your ICOCA. The trail starts directly behind the station's shopping street.",cost:"~$15/person (trains ~$3.60 + momiji tempura ¥400/$2.50 + coffee; lunch back in Umeda)",hours:"09:00–14:00",tier:3,pitch:"Osaka's hidden reset button: a 33-meter waterfall at the end of a gentle 2.8km river-gorge trail, half an hour from Umeda for less than two dollars. The signature snack is momiji tempura — actual maple leaves, battered and fried, a tradition the trailside shops have kept going for centuries. December means a near-empty trail, maybe the last stubborn red maples, and a full afternoon still left in the city.",play:["09:00 — Hankyu from Umeda, cross-platform change at Ishibashi handai-mae","09:40 — Minoh station: grab coffee, hit the paved riverside trail behind the shopping street","10:00 — Momiji tempura stalls along the lower trail — buy a bag of fried maple leaves for the walk (sweet, crunchy, weirdly addictive)","10:30 — Ryuanji (Mino Temple) halfway up: vermillion bridge over the gorge, founded 7th century","11:15 — Minoo Falls: 33m of water in a maple amphitheater — winter flow against bare red-brown hillsides, photo, snack, exhale","12:00 — Amble back down; the tiny Minoh insect museum (¥280/$1.80) for the brave, beer-of-record at Minoh Beer's local taps if open","13:00 — Hankyu back — standing in Umeda by ~13:45 with the whole Osaka afternoon ahead"],protip:"Wild monkeys live in the park — don't eat while walking and don't flash the tempura bag, they know exactly what's in it. The trail is paved the whole way (sneakers fine, zero hiking gear), which makes this the perfect hangover-morning or rest-day half-card; pair it with Umeda Sky Building at sunset for a full low-effort day.",links:[{label:"Minoo Park official",url:"https://www.mino-park.jp/"},{label:"Hankyu Railway (English)",url:"https://www.hankyu.co.jp/global/"}],wiki:"Meiji no Mori Minō Quasi-National Park"},{id:"koyasan",name:"Koyasan — Mountain of the Dead",jp:"高野山",base:"Kansai",travel:"All on ICOCA, no rail pass: Nankai Koya line limited express Osaka-Namba→Gokurakubashi, ~80 min, ¥1,650 ($10) each way (¥870 fare + ¥780 ltd-express seat), then the 5-min funicular cable car up the mountainside (¥500/$3.10) and a bus into town (tap your card). The Nankai 'Koyasan World Heritage Ticket' bundles the round trip + buses if you'd rather one purchase — your call, but plain IC works fine.",cost:"~$55/person (trains+cable+bus ~$32 + Kongobuji ¥1,000/$6.30 + shojin-ryori lunch + Okunoin free)",hours:"07:30–19:30",tier:3,pitch:"1,200 years of Shingon Buddhism on a misty mountaintop temple-town at 800m. The headliner is Okunoin: a 2km path through Japan's largest cemetery — 200,000+ moss-furred graves and towering cedars leading to the mausoleum of Kobo Daishi, who is officially 'meditating,' not dead, and gets two meals a day delivered to this morning. December buries the whole place in silence, often snow, and almost no one else. Heaviest atmosphere of any day in the deck.",play:["07:30 — Nankai limited express from Namba (reserve the seat; mountains close in fast through the windows)","09:00 — Gokurakubashi → cable car up the cliff (steep enough to feel it) → bus into town","09:40 — Danjo Garan: the sacred temple complex and the vermillion Konpon Daito great pagoda, the spiritual heart of the mountain","10:45 — Kongobuji (¥1,000/$6.30): head temple of Shingon, gilded sliding-door paintings and Japan's largest rock garden (Banryutei)","12:00 — Shojin-ryori lunch: Buddhist vegetarian temple cuisine (sesame tofu, mountain vegetables, ~¥2,000/$13) — the real Koyasan meal, hot food at altitude","13:30 — Okunoin: enter at Ichi-no-hashi and walk the full 2km cemetery path through giant cedars and 200,000 graves to the Torodo lantern hall — no photos past the last bridge (Gobyobashi), out of respect","15:30 — Daimon, the great mountain gate, and warm amazake at a tea house to thaw","16:30 — Cable car + Nankai limited express back; Namba by ~18:30, recalibrated"],protip:"It is the single coldest place on the Kansai roster — a snowy mountaintop, dawn well below freezing, paths slick with ice and packed snow; bring genuine winter layers and grip soles, this is not an Osaka-street outfit. The Okunoin path is worth doing in fading December light (lantern-lit, deeply atmospheric) but be off the mountain before the last comfortable cable car. If a temple stay (shukubo) ever tempted you, this is the place — but as a day trip it still lands hard.",links:[{label:"Koyasan Shingon Buddhism (official)",url:"https://www.koyasan.or.jp/en/"},{label:"Nankai Railway — Koyasan",url:"https://www.howto-osaka.com/en/ticket/details/koyasan_worldheritage/"}],wiki:"Mount Kōya"},{id:"kurama-kibune",name:"Kurama → Kibune — Mountain Onsen Hike",jp:"鞍馬・貴船",base:"Kansai",travel:"All on ICOCA, no pass: Keihan main line to Demachiyanagi, then the two-car Eizan 'Eiden' mountain railway Demachiyanagi→Kurama, ~30 min, ¥470 ($2.90) — sit on the right for the cedar gorge. Hop off one stop earlier at Kibune-guchi on the way back; total day's rail is well under ¥2,000 ($13). Winter Eizan service thins out, so screenshot the timetable.",cost:"~$45/person (trains ~$13 + Kurama-dera ¥500/$3.10 + Kurama Onsen rotenburo ¥1,300/$8 + kaiseki or noodles)",hours:"08:30–17:30",tier:3,pitch:"Thirty minutes north of the city, Kyoto turns into a snow-dusted cedar mountain. Climb from Kurama-dera temple over the pass — through the gnarled exposed tree roots where the tengu mountain goblins supposedly trained the boy who became Japan's greatest warrior — and down into Kibune, a shrine village of lantern-lit ryokan stacked along a rushing stream. Then strip down at Kurama Onsen's open-air rotenburo and soak chin-deep facing a forested slope while snow falls on your head. The most nature, and the best bath, of any Kansai day.",play:["08:30 — Keihan to Demachiyanagi, change to the little Eizan railway (front seats, gorge windows)","09:15 — Kurama station: the giant red tengu face out front, then the cedar-lined approach up the village street","09:45 — Kurama-dera (¥500/$3.10): ride the short cable car or climb to the main hall and its mountain-energy 'power spot' triangle in the pavement, valley views opening below","10:45 — The mountain trail over the pass toward Kibune — past the tree-root slope (kinone-michi) where Minamoto no Yoshitsune trained with the tengu, ~45–60 min, real shoes required","12:00 — Drop into Kibune: the vermillion-lantern stone steps of Kifune Shrine (water-god shrine; float a paper fortune in the sacred spring to reveal the writing)","12:45 — Lunch in Kibune — winter is botan-nabe (wild-boar hot pot) or kaiseki in a lantern-lit ryokan dining room over the iced-over stream (~¥3,000–5,000/$19–31), the warm reward for the hike","14:30 — Eizan back one stop's worth to Kurama Onsen: the open-air rotenburo facing the forested slope (¥1,300/$8) — soaking in snow-flecked outdoor water is the entire point of coming in December","16:00 — Thaw, dress, and ride the Eiden back down; Demachiyanagi and central Kyoto by ~17:15"],protip:"This is a genuine cold mountain, 5–8°C below central Kyoto with ice and packed snow on the pass trail — grip soles and real layers, not your Gion sightseeing outfit. If the trail is closed or anyone's not up for the climb, skip it and ride the Eizan two stops between Kurama and Kibune-guchi, then walk the valley road — you still get both villages and the bath. Check the Eizan winter timetable and the last comfortable downhill train; service is sparse and the village empties early in winter.",links:[{label:"Eizan Railway (English)",url:"https://eizandensha.co.jp/en/"},{label:"Kuramadera Temple",url:"https://www.kuramadera.or.jp/"},{label:"Kurama Onsen",url:"https://www.kurama-onsen.co.jp/"}],wiki:"Kurama-dera"},{id:"kinosaki-onsen",name:"Kinosaki Onsen — Seven-Bath Crawl",jp:"城崎温泉",base:"Kansai",travel:"Direct on a reserved limited express, no pass: JR Limited Express 'Kinosaki' Kyoto→Kinosaki-Onsen, ~2h30, ~¥4,840 ($30) reserved each way (the train splits/runs straight up the San'in line — buy seats at the machine or on the JR West site); or the 'Kounotori' from Shin-Osaka, ~2h40, ~¥6,140 ($38). The town is tiny and entirely walkable from the station — no buses, no transfers.",cost:"~$80/person (train ~$60 + all-seven-bath day pass ¥1,500/$9.40 + crab/tajima-beef lunch)",hours:"08:30–20:30",tier:3,pitch:"A willow-lined canal town on the Sea of Japan coast where the whole point is to walk the streets in a yukata and wooden geta, clogs clacking, drifting between seven public hot-spring bathhouses with a single pass. December is the headline season: this is the snow-crab capital of Japan, the steam rises thick off the canal in the cold, and a fresh snowfall on the lantern-lit streets is the postcard. The most pure-relaxation day in the entire deck, and a long, scenic train nap to get there.",play:["08:30 — JR Limited Express Kinosaki from Kyoto (reserve seats together; coffee and a long, scenic nap up through the mountains and out to the coast)","11:00 — Kinosaki-Onsen station: a free public foot bath and a hot-spring drinking fountain greet you on the platform; grab the ¥1,500/$9.40 'Yumepa' all-bath day pass","11:20 — Check a bag at the station and change into the rental yukata + geta — wearing it town-wide is the tradition, and it's free or cheap from most shops","11:45 — Bath #1, Satonoyu by the station (the biggest — top-floor open-air baths and a 'penguin' cold sauna) to start the crawl warm","13:00 — Crab lunch: December is matsuba (snow) crab season and Kinosaki is the source — a whole grilled/boiled crab set, or Tajima beef if crab prices scare the group (~¥3,000–6,000/$19–38)","14:30 — Stroll the willow-lined canal, clacking in geta over the little stone bridges — bath-hop through Ichinoyu (cave bath), Goshonoyu (waterfall bath), and Mandaranoyu as you go","16:30 — December dusk and the canal lanterns light up; the Kinosaki Ropeway up Mt. Daishi for a snow-coast panorama if anyone has bath-energy left (~¥900/$5.60)","17:30 — A final soak, onsen-pudding and crab croquettes from the canal stalls, then the limited express back — Kyoto by ~20:30"],protip:"Buy the round-trip limited express seats in advance — December weekend trains to crab country fill up, and reserved is worth it for 2.5 hours each way. Tattoos are generally fine at the public bathhouses here (unusually relaxed), but confirm at the door. It's a long day for a town this small, so it's a tier-3 audible: ideal as the trip's designated do-nothing recovery day, not a sightseeing sprint. The crab is the splurge — set a per-person budget before you sit down.",links:[{label:"Visit Kinosaki (official, English)",url:"https://visitkinosaki.com/"},{label:"JR West timetable & fares",url:"https://www.westjr.co.jp/global/en/"}],wiki:"Kinosaki Onsen"},{id:"kanazawa",name:"Kanazawa — Little Kyoto of the North",jp:"金沢",base:"Kansai",travel:"Reserved, no pass, one easy transfer: JR Limited Express 'Thunderbird' Kyoto→Tsuruga ~45 min, cross-platform change to the Hokuriku Shinkansen 'Tsurugi/Hakutaka' Tsuruga→Kanazawa ~1h — ~2h door to door total, ~¥7,500 ($47) reserved each way. Book the through-ticket on the JR West site; the Tsuruga transfer is signposted and takes minutes. In town, the ¥200/$1.30 flat-fare Kanazawa Loop Bus (tap ICOCA) rings every sight.",cost:"~$130/person (trains ~$94 + Kenrokuen ¥320/$2 + castle + 21st Century Museum ¥450/$2.80 + seafood-bowl lunch)",hours:"07:30–20:30",tier:3,pitch:"The castle town the war never touched, two hours north on the bullet train: Kenrokuen, ranked one of Japan's three greatest gardens, plus an immaculate samurai district, a geisha quarter of preserved teahouses, gold-leaf everything (the city makes 99% of Japan's), and an Edo-era market piled with Sea-of-Japan winter crab and shrimp. December dresses Kenrokuen in 'yukitsuri' — the conical rope umbrellas that protect the pines from snow — which is the single most beautiful thing in any Japanese garden in winter. A long but spectacular full-day audible from the Kyoto base.",play:["07:30 — Kyoto: Thunderbird to Tsuruga, cross-platform to the Hokuriku Shinkansen for Kanazawa (reserve the through-ticket; breakfast bento on board)","09:30 — Kanazawa station: gawk at the colossal wooden Tsuzumi-mon 'drum gate,' then the ¥200 Loop Bus toward the center","10:00 — Kenrokuen Garden (¥320/$2): the December 'yukitsuri' snow-rope cones strung over every pine, the Kotojitoro lantern, frosted ponds — Japan's garden art at its absolute winter peak","11:15 — Kanazawa Castle next door: the white-lead-tile roofs and the rebuilt Hishi Yagura turret, gardens and gate free to roam","12:00 — Omicho Market: the Edo-era covered market — a kaisendon piled with snow crab, sweet shrimp, and sea urchin (~¥2,500/$16), December being peak Sea-of-Japan season","13:30 — Higashi Chaya District: the preserved geisha-teahouse quarter — gold-leaf ice cream (yes, edible gold), the Kaikaro teahouse, lacquer and craft shops in dark wooden machiya","14:45 — Nagamachi samurai district: earthen-walled lanes and the Nomura-ke samurai residence with its tiny perfect garden, a short Loop Bus hop south","15:45 — 21st Century Museum of Contemporary Art (¥450/$2.80 for exhibits; Leandro Erlich's walk-on 'Swimming Pool' is the icon) — a glassy, modern palate-cleanser","17:00 — Last gold-leaf souvenir run near the station, then the shinkansen + Thunderbird back; Kyoto by ~20:30"],protip:"Book the through reserved ticket in advance — the Tsuruga transfer is painless but the trains run reserved-heavy and December fills them. It's the longest single-day rail haul on the Kansai roster (~4h round trip), so it lives as a tier-3 'if a full day frees up' play, not a casual one. Kanazawa is famously the rainiest/snowiest city — 'forget your umbrella' is a local saying — so pack waterproofs and grip soles; Kenrokuen under actual snow is the jackpot but the paths get slick.",links:[{label:"Visit Kanazawa (official)",url:"https://visitkanazawa.jp/en/"},{label:"Kenrokuen Garden (official)",url:"https://www.pref.ishikawa.jp/siro-niwa/kenrokuen/e/"},{label:"JR West — Hokuriku & Thunderbird",url:"https://www.westjr.co.jp/global/en/"}],wiki:"Kanazawa"},{id:"amanohashidate-ine",name:"Amanohashidate + Ine no Funaya",jp:"天橋立・伊根の舟屋",base:"Kansai",travel:"Reserved, no pass: the JR/Kyoto Tango Railway Limited Express 'Hashidate' Kyoto→Amanohashidate, ~2h, ~¥4,400 ($28) reserved each way (runs onto the private Tango line — buy the through reserved seat). From Amanohashidate, the Tankai bus up to Ine is ~1h, ¥400/$2.50 (tap or cash); the village bay-tour boat loops from there. Long day, but one scenic train each way.",cost:"~$80/person (train ~$56 + chairlift ¥850/$5.30 + Ine bay boat ¥1,000/$6.30 + Ine bus + seafood lunch)",hours:"07:30–20:00",tier:3,pitch:"Two of the Sea of Japan's quietest postcards in one big day. Amanohashidate is one of Japan's three official 'great views' — a 3.6km pine sandbar that, viewed upside-down between your legs from the hilltop, becomes a 'bridge to heaven.' Then a coastal bus to Ine, where 230 wooden 'boat houses' stand directly over the bay, boat garage below and home above, best seen from a little tour boat with gulls dive-bombing for crackers. Remote, cold, crowd-free, and unlike anywhere else on the trip.",play:["07:30 — Kyoto: Limited Express Hashidate north onto the Tango Railway (reserve seats; long scenic mountain-and-coast nap)","09:35 — Amanohashidate station: walk out onto the pine sandbar itself, or rent bikes to cross its 3.6km","10:30 — Kasamatsu Park via chairlift/cablecar (¥850/$5.30 round trip) for the famous 'matanozoki' — bend over, look between your legs, watch the sandbar float into the sky; undignified and mandatory","11:30 — Tankai bus up the coast to Ine (~1h) — Sea-of-Japan views the whole way","12:45 — Ine bay sightseeing boat (~¥1,000/$6.30): loop the bay to see the full row of 230 funaya boat houses from the water, gulls and black kites chasing the shrimp crackers","13:45 — Seafood lunch in Ine — whatever the morning boats landed, plus a wander past the funaya at water level","15:00 — Bus back to Amanohashidate; a last sandbar stroll or a hot drink as the winter light goes flat and gold","16:30 — Limited Express Hashidate back to Kyoto by ~20:00"],protip:"This is a real expedition — far north, genuinely cold and windy on the winter coast, and the Ine buses and bay boats run a thin December schedule, so screenshot the timetables and build the day around the boat departure, not the other way round. Reserve the limited express both ways; the Tango line runs short trains. Pure tier-3: only worth it if a full clear day frees up and the group wants remote-and-quiet over another temple sprint.",links:[{label:"Amanohashidate tourism (official)",url:"https://www.amanohashidate.jp/en/"},{label:"Ine Tourism (funaya)",url:"https://www.ine-kankou.jp/en/"},{label:"Kyoto Tango Railway (English)",url:"https://trains.willer.co.jp/en/"}],wiki:"Amanohashidate"}];export{e as A,t as D,a as N};
